CONTENT_EN.expr = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to write a word problem's conditions in the language of mathematics — as a numerical or literal expression, read an order-of-operations plan, and solve word problems step by step or with a single expression.",
  explain: "<p>Mathematics has its own language: instead of long phrases, it writes short records made of numbers, letters, and operation signs. Once you learn this language, you'll be able to &ldquo;translate&rdquo; any word problem into mathematical form, and then just calculate.</p>"
  + "<h3>A numerical expression and its value</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>numerical expression</b> is a record made of numbers, operation signs, and (sometimes) parentheses. For example: 15 + 8, 40 − 3 · 6, (12 + 8) : 5. Even a single number is also a numerical expression. The result of the calculation is called the <b>value of the expression</b>: the value of 15 + 8 is 23, and the value of 40 − 3 · 6 is 22 (first 3 · 6 = 18, then 40 − 18).</p>"
  + "<p>An expression can be named by its <b>last operation</b>. The expression 15 + 8 is the sum of the numbers 15 and 8. The expression (12 + 8) : 5 is the quotient of the sum of 12 and 8 and the number 5, because the last operation performed is division. And 40 − 3 · 6 is the difference of the number 40 and the product of the numbers 3 and 6.</p>"
  + "<p>It helps to know a &ldquo;translator&rdquo; from everyday words: &ldquo;7 more&rdquo; means add 7; &ldquo;7 less&rdquo; means subtract 7; &ldquo;3 times more&rdquo; means multiply by 3; &ldquo;3 times less&rdquo; means divide by 3; &ldquo;in total,&rdquo; &ldquo;altogether&rdquo; usually mean addition; &ldquo;left&rdquo; means subtraction; &ldquo;5 items in each of 4 boxes&rdquo; means the multiplication 5 · 4.</p>"
  + "<h3>An order-of-operations plan</h3>"
  + "<p>To calculate a long expression, we write out an <b>order-of-operations plan</b> — a list of steps in the order they must be done. You already know the order: parentheses first, then multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction (covered in detail in the order-of-operations topic). Let's take the expression (8 + 9) · 2 − 5 · 5:</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_EXPR_PROGRAM__</div>"
  + "<p>In the diagram, the steps are numbered with circles ①②③④. Parentheses are done first: 8 + 9 = 17. Then 17 · 2 = 34, and independently, 5 · 5 = 25. The last step is subtraction: 34 − 25 = 9. So the value of the expression is 9. Notice: the last operation here is subtraction, so the expression reads as &ldquo;the difference of the product of the sum of 8 and 9 and the number 2, and the product of the numbers 5 and 5.&rdquo;</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>An order-of-operations plan is a numbered list written in order: &ldquo;1) …, 2) …, 3) ….&rdquo; The number of steps in it equals the number of operation signs in the expression. The expression (8 + 9) · 2 − 5 · 5 has four signs (+, ·, −, ·), so it has four steps.</div>"
  + "<h3>A literal expression and substitution</h3>"
  + "<p>If a number in a record can change, it's replaced with a <b>letter</b>. The expression a + 5 reads &ldquo;the sum of the number a and five&rdquo;; the expression 3 · b reads &ldquo;triple b,&rdquo; and can be written more briefly as 3b. The multiplication sign between a number and a letter (and between letters) can be dropped: 3b, ab. But it can't be dropped between two numbers: 3 · 5 is not 35!</p>"
  + "<p>To find the value of a literal expression, you need to <b>substitute</b> a number in place of the letter. Let a = 4. Then the value of 3a + 7 is 3 · 4 + 7 = 12 + 7 = 19. At a = 10, you get 3 · 10 + 7 = 37. One record — many different values: that's the power of letters.</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>When substituting a number for a letter, kids often forget the multiplication sign. If a = 4, then 3a is 3 · 4 = 12, not &ldquo;34&rdquo; at all. Substitute and immediately write the &ldquo;·&rdquo; sign between the number and the substituted value.</div>"
  + "<h3>How to write an expression from a word problem</h3>"
  + "<p>Problem: &ldquo;A store had 200 kg of apples. In the morning, x kg were sold, and in the afternoon, another y kg. How many kilograms of apples are left?&rdquo; Let's read it slowly: there were 200, x were sold, then y more were sold — so what's left is 200 − x − y. This is a literal expression. If x = 70 and y = 45, then what's left is 200 − 70 − 45 = 85 kg. The same record works for any sales figures.</p>"
  + "<h3>Word problems and the bar diagram</h3>"
  + "<p>Complex problems are convenient to draw as a <b>bar</b>: the whole quantity is a long bar, and its parts are smaller bars of the same &ldquo;unit&rdquo; length. Let's work through the problem: &ldquo;Sergey, Kostya, and Denis brought in 120 stamps. Sergey brought 25 stamps, Kostya brought twice as many as Sergey. How many stamps did Denis bring?&rdquo;</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_EXPR_BAR_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<p>To find the unknown part, we subtract the known parts from the whole. This kind of problem can be solved <b>step by step</b> (1) 25 · 2 = 50 — Kostya; 2) 25 + 50 = 75 — two of them; 3) 120 − 75 = 45 — Denis) or <b>with a single expression</b>: 120 − (25 + 25 · 2) = 45. The answer, of course, is the same either way.</p>"
  + "<h3>Convenient calculation: properties of operations</h3>"
  + "<p>Sometimes an expression can be calculated cleverly, without computing it &ldquo;head-on.&rdquo; Three properties help with this. <b>Commutative:</b> a + b = b + a and a · b = b · a — addends and factors can be swapped. <b>Associative:</b> (a + b) + c = a + (b + c) and (a · b) · c = a · (b · c) — you can group them however is convenient. <b>Distributive:</b> a · (b + c) = a · b + a · c.</p>"
  + "<p>Examples: 38 + 57 + 62 = (38 + 62) + 57 = 100 + 57 = 157. Or: 25 · 37 · 4 = (25 · 4) · 37 = 100 · 37 = 3,700. Or: 46 · 8 + 46 · 2 = 46 · (8 + 2) = 46 · 10 = 460. Look for &ldquo;round&rdquo; pairs: 25 and 4, 50 and 2, 125 and 8, addends that give 10, 100, 1,000.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Three steps to solving a problem: 1) read it and identify what's known and what you need to find; 2) write a plan — step by step or as an expression, optionally drawing a bar diagram; 3) calculate and check that the answer fits the situation (you shouldn't get &ldquo;−5 apples&rdquo; or &ldquo;2.5 students&rdquo;).</div>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Write an order-of-operations plan and calculate: 100 − 3 · (12 + 8) + 6 : 2.", s:"Parentheses first: 1) 12 + 8 = 20. Then multiplication and division, left to right: 2) 3 · 20 = 60; 3) 6 : 2 = 3. What's left are addition and subtraction, also left to right: 4) 100 − 60 = 40; 5) 40 + 3 = 43. The expression has five operation signs — and five steps in the plan. The value of the expression is 43."},
    {t:"Read the expression by its last operation and find its value: (18 + 12) : (10 − 4).", s:"The last operation is division. We read it: the quotient of the sum of 18 and 12 and the difference of 10 and 4. Plan: 1) 18 + 12 = 30; 2) 10 − 4 = 6; 3) 30 : 6 = 5. The value of the expression is 5."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 3a + 7 at a = 6 and at a = 0.", s:"At a = 6: 3 · 6 + 7 = 18 + 7 = 25. At a = 0: 3 · 0 + 7 = 0 + 7 = 7. Don't forget: 3a is multiplication, not &ldquo;the number 36.&rdquo;"},
    {t:"Write an expression: a box had 200 pencils, a of them were taken out, then b were added. How many are there now? Find the value at a = 60, b = 25.", s:"There were 200, a were taken out — leaving 200 − a. Then b were added: 200 − a + b. At a = 60, b = 25: 200 − 60 + 25 = 140 + 25 = 165 pencils."},
    {t:"Three boxes hold 100 pencils in total. The first has 24, the second has 8 more than the first. How many pencils are in the third box?", s:"Bar diagram: the whole bar is 100, the parts are 24, 24 + 8, and &ldquo;?&rdquo;. Step by step: 1) 24 + 8 = 32 — in the second; 2) 24 + 32 = 56 — in the two boxes; 3) 100 − 56 = 44 — in the third. As a single expression: 100 − 24 − (24 + 8) = 44. Answer: 44 pencils."},
    {t:"Find the mistake: a student worked out 8 + 2 · 5 = 50. What's the mistake, and what's the correct answer?", s:"The student did the operations left to right: 8 + 2 = 10, 10 · 5 = 50. But multiplication is done before addition: 1) 2 · 5 = 10; 2) 8 + 10 = 18. The correct answer is 18. (To get 50, you'd need parentheses: (8 + 2) · 5.)"},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 25 · 37 · 4 and 38 + 57 + 62.", s:"25 · 37 · 4 = (25 · 4) · 37 = 100 · 37 = 3,700. 38 + 57 + 62 = (38 + 62) + 57 = 100 + 57 = 157. We swapped the addends and factors (commutative property) and grouped round pairs (associative property)."},
    {t:"Find the value of x for which the value of the expression 3x + 7 equals 37.", s:"We need 3x = 37 − 7 = 30. So x = 30 : 3 = 10. Check: 3 · 10 + 7 = 37. Correct, the answer is 10."}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 24 + 6 · 5.", ans:"54", hint:"Multiplication is done before addition.", sol:"1) 6 · 5 = 30; 2) 24 + 30 = 54."},
    {q:"How many steps are in the plan for the expression 7 · 3 + 40 : 8 − 2?", ans:"4", hint:"The number of steps equals the number of operation signs in the record.", sol:"The record has four signs: ·, +, :, −. So the plan has four steps: 7 · 3 = 21; 40 : 8 = 5; 21 + 5 = 26; 26 − 2 = 24."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 5a + 3 at a = 4.", ans:"23", hint:"Substitute the number for the letter and don't forget the multiplication sign.", sol:"5 · 4 + 3 = 20 + 3 = 23."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression &ldquo;the difference of the number 100 and the product of the numbers 7 and 9.&rdquo;", ans:"37", hint:"Write the expression in digits: the last operation is subtraction.", sol:"The expression: 100 − 7 · 9. 1) 7 · 9 = 63; 2) 100 − 63 = 37."},
    {q:"A pastry costs p rubles, a cup of tea costs t rubles. Which expression gives the cost of 3 pastries and one cup of tea?", opts:["3 · p + t","3 · (p + t)","p + t + 3","3 + p · t"], ans:0, hint:"There are three pastries, but only one tea: what gets multiplied only by p?", sol:"Three pastries cost 3 · p, tea costs t. In total: 3 · p + t. The expression 3 · (p + t) would mean 3 pastries and 3 cups of tea."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 4x − 9 at x = 12.", ans:"39", hint:"First substitute, then multiply, then subtract.", sol:"4 · 12 − 9 = 48 − 9 = 39."},
    {q:"A notebook costs 18 rubles, a pen costs 7 rubles more than the notebook. How many rubles do 2 notebooks and a pen cost?", ans:"61", hint:"First find the price of the pen.", sol:"1) 18 + 7 = 25 — the pen's price; 2) 2 · 18 = 36 — two notebooks; 3) 36 + 25 = 61. As a single expression: 2 · 18 + (18 + 7) = 61."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression (a + b) · c − a at a = 6, b = 9, c = 4.", ans:"54", hint:"Substitute all three numbers, then do the parentheses, multiplication, subtraction.", sol:"(6 + 9) · 4 − 6: 1) 6 + 9 = 15; 2) 15 · 4 = 60; 3) 60 − 6 = 54."},
    {q:"A store received 3 crates of apples at 24 kg each and 2 crates of pears at 18 kg each. How many kilograms of fruit were delivered?", ans:"108", hint:"Find the weight of the apples and the weight of the pears separately.", sol:"1) 3 · 24 = 72 kg of apples; 2) 2 · 18 = 36 kg of pears; 3) 72 + 36 = 108. As an expression: 3 · 24 + 2 · 18 = 108."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 4 · 17 · 25.", ans:"1700", hint:"Which two factors give a product of 100?", sol:"4 · 17 · 25 = (4 · 25) · 17 = 100 · 17 = 1,700."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 38 + 57 + 62 + 43.", ans:"200", hint:"Find the addends that add up to 100.", sol:"(38 + 62) + (57 + 43) = 100 + 100 = 200."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression using the distributive property: 46 · 8 + 46 · 2.", ans:"460", hint:"Factor out the common factor 46.", sol:"46 · 8 + 46 · 2 = 46 · (8 + 2) = 46 · 10 = 460."},
    {q:"Anya, Vera, and Galya gathered 90 mushrooms. Anya gathered 24 mushrooms, Vera gathered twice as many as Anya. How many mushrooms did Galya gather?", ans:"18", hint:"Draw a bar of 90 and mark on it the parts for Anya, Vera, and Galya.", sol:"1) 24 · 2 = 48 — Vera; 2) 24 + 48 = 72 — Anya and Vera; 3) 90 − 72 = 18. As an expression: 90 − 24 − 24 · 2 = 18."},
    {q:"For what value of x is the value of the expression 5x + 12 equal to 87?", ans:"15", hint:"First subtract 12 from 87 and think about what x was multiplied by.", sol:"5x = 87 − 12 = 75; x = 75 : 5 = 15. Check: 5 · 15 + 12 = 87."},
    {q:"What is the value of the expression 60 − 4 · 5 + 3?", opts:["283","37","43","28"], ans:2, hint:"Multiplication comes before subtraction and addition, then left to right.", sol:"1) 4 · 5 = 20; 2) 60 − 20 = 40; 3) 40 + 3 = 43. The answer 283 comes from calculating everything left to right, 37 from adding 20 + 3 first and then subtracting, and 28 from inserting parentheses as 4 · (5 + 3)."},
    {q:"3 pens at 15 rubles each and 4 notebooks at 22 rubles each were bought. They paid with two 100-ruble bills. How many rubles of change did they get?", ans:"67", hint:"Find the cost of the purchase, then subtract it from the amount paid.", sol:"1) 3 · 15 = 45 (pens); 2) 4 · 22 = 88 (notebooks); 3) 45 + 88 = 133 — the cost; 4) 2 · 100 = 200 — the amount paid; 5) 200 − 133 = 67. As an expression: 2 · 100 − (3 · 15 + 4 · 22) = 67."},
    {q:"Three sacks hold 130 kg of potatoes in total. The first sack has 40 kg, the second has twice as much as the first. How many kilograms of potatoes are in the third sack?", ans:"10", hint:"The second sack is 40 · 2. Subtract both known sacks from the total.", sol:"1) 40 · 2 = 80 kg in the second; 2) 40 + 80 = 120 kg in the two sacks; 3) 130 − 120 = 10 kg. As an expression: 130 − 40 − 40 · 2 = 10."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 2a + 3b − ab at a = 5 and b = 4.", ans:"2", hint:"ab is a · b. Substitute with multiplication signs: 2 · 5 + 3 · 4 − 5 · 4.", sol:"2 · 5 + 3 · 4 − 5 · 4 = 10 + 12 − 20 = 2."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 125 · 7 · 8 − 64 · 25 · 4.", ans:"600", hint:"In the first product, group 125 and 8; in the second, group 25 and 4.", sol:"125 · 7 · 8 = (125 · 8) · 7 = 1,000 · 7 = 7,000. 64 · 25 · 4 = 64 · (25 · 4) = 64 · 100 = 6,400. The difference: 7,000 − 6,400 = 600."},
    {q:"Mom bought 5 identical cartons of juice and 3 buns at 12 rubles each. She paid 236 rubles for everything. How many rubles does one carton of juice cost?", ans:"40", hint:"First find out how much the buns cost, then how much was paid for the juice.", sol:"1) 3 · 12 = 36 — the buns; 2) 236 − 36 = 200 — 5 cartons of juice; 3) 200 : 5 = 40. As an expression: (236 − 3 · 12) : 5 = 40."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 8a − 15 at a = 9.", ans:"57", hint:"First substitute the number for the letter, then multiply, then subtract at the end.", sol:"8 · 9 − 15 = 72 − 15 = 57."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression &ldquo;the sum of the quotient of the numbers 96 and 8 and the product of the numbers 5 and 7.&rdquo;", ans:"47", hint:"Write the expression in digits: 96 : 8 + 5 · 7.", sol:"96 : 8 + 5 · 7: 1) 96 : 8 = 12; 2) 5 · 7 = 35; 3) 12 + 35 = 47."},
    {q:"Mom bought 4 packs of cookies at 35 rubles each and a cake for 180 rubles. She paid with a 500-ruble bill. How many rubles of change did she get?", ans:"180", hint:"First find the cost of the whole purchase, then subtract it from 500 rubles.", sol:"1) 4 · 35 = 140 (cookies); 2) 140 + 180 = 320 (the whole purchase); 3) 500 − 320 = 180. As an expression: 500 − (4 · 35 + 180) = 180."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 250 · 18 · 4.", ans:"18000", hint:"Find two factors that give a round number when multiplied.", sol:"250 · 18 · 4 = (250 · 4) · 18 = 1,000 · 18 = 18,000."},
    {q:"A pencil costs x rubles, and a sketchbook costs 45 rubles more than the pencil. 2 pencils and a sketchbook cost 120 rubles. How many rubles does the pencil cost?", ans:"25", hint:"Write the cost of the purchase in terms of x: two pencils plus the sketchbook (x + 45).", sol:"2x + (x + 45) = 120, that is, 3x + 45 = 120. 3x = 75, x = 25. Check: the sketchbook is 70 rubles, 2 · 25 + 70 = 120. Answer: 25."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a rectangle is found by the formula P = 2 · (a + b). Find the side b (in cm) if P = 56 cm, a = 17 cm.", ans:"11", hint:"Since P is double the sum of the sides, the sum a + b is half the perimeter.", sol:"a + b = 56 : 2 = 28 cm. b = 28 − 17 = 11 cm. Answer: 11."},
    {q:"Vasya found the value of the expression 7a − 2b at a = 6 and b = 5 like this: 7 · 6 − 2 · 5 = 42 − 2 · 5 = 40 · 5 = 200. Find the mistake and calculate the correct value.", ans:"32", hint:"Which operation should be done first: subtraction or multiplication?", sol:"Vasya subtracted before multiplying. Correct: 7 · 6 = 42; 2 · 5 = 10; 42 − 10 = 32. Answer: 32."},
    {q:"A cyclist rode for 3 hours at a speed of 14 km/h, and then for another 2 hours at a speed of 12 km/h. How many kilometers did he travel in total?", ans:"66", hint:"Distance = speed · time. Find the distance on each leg separately.", sol:"1) 14 · 3 = 42 km. 2) 12 · 2 = 24 km. 3) 42 + 24 = 66 km. Answer: 66."},
    {q:"Two brothers saved 480 rubles together, with the older one having 3 times as much money as the younger one. How many rubles does the older brother have?", ans:"360", hint:"If the younger one's money is one part, how many such parts does the older one have, and how many in total?", sol:"The younger one is 1 part, the older one is 3 parts, 4 parts in total. 1) 480 : 4 = 120 rubles for the younger one. 2) 120 · 3 = 360 rubles for the older one. Answer: 360."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression 7x + 5x − 3x and find its value at x = 8.", ans:"72", hint:"Factor out x: (7 + 5 − 3) · x.", sol:"7x + 5x − 3x = (7 + 5 − 3) · x = 9x. At x = 8: 9 · 8 = 72. Answer: 72."},
    {q:"A bus traveled for t hours at a speed of 60 km/h, and then for another 2 hours at a speed of 50 km/h. Which expression shows the bus's whole trip (in km)?", opts:["60t + 100","60 · (t + 2)","110t","60 + t + 100"], ans:0, hint:"Find the distance on each leg separately: multiply speed by time.", sol:"First leg: 60 · t = 60t km. Second: 50 · 2 = 100 km. The whole trip: 60t + 100. Answer: 60t + 100."},
    {q:"I thought of a number, added 7 to it, multiplied the result by 3, and got 72. What number did I think of?", ans:"17", hint:"Work backward: which operation undoes multiplication, and which undoes addition?", sol:"(x + 7) · 3 = 72. x + 7 = 72 : 3 = 24. x = 24 − 7 = 17. Check: (17 + 7) · 3 = 72. Answer: 17."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"Find the value of the expression 2 + 4 + 6 + … + 100 − (1 + 3 + 5 + … + 99). The first sum has all the even numbers from 2 to 100, the second has all the odd numbers from 1 to 99.", ans:"50", hint:"Don't calculate the sums separately. Group the numbers in pairs: 2 and 1, 4 and 3, 6 and 5, …", sol:"Let's write it as (2 − 1) + (4 − 3) + (6 − 5) + … + (100 − 99). In each pair, the difference equals 1. There are as many pairs as there are even numbers from 2 to 100, that is, 100 : 2 = 50. So the value of the expression is 50 · 1 = 50."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 15 + 6 · (20 − 14).", ans:"51", sol:"1) 20 − 14 = 6; 2) 6 · 6 = 36; 3) 15 + 36 = 51."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 6a − 15 at a = 7.", ans:"27", sol:"6 · 7 − 15 = 42 − 15 = 27."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression &ldquo;the sum of the product of the numbers 8 and 7 and the quotient of the numbers 72 and 9.&rdquo;", ans:"64", sol:"8 · 7 + 72 : 9: 1) 8 · 7 = 56; 2) 72 : 9 = 8; 3) 56 + 8 = 64."},
    {q:"There are three packs of 24 notebooks each. 15 notebooks were given out to students. How many notebooks are left?", ans:"57", sol:"1) 3 · 24 = 72; 2) 72 − 15 = 57. As a single expression: 3 · 24 − 15 = 57."},
    {q:"The number x was decreased by a factor of 4, and the result was increased by 9. Which expression records this?", opts:["x · 4 + 9","x : 4 + 9","x − 4 + 9","(x + 9) : 4"], ans:1, sol:"&ldquo;Decrease by a factor of 4&rdquo; means divide by 4: x : 4. &ldquo;Increase by 9&rdquo; means add 9. We get x : 4 + 9."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression the convenient way: 5 · 47 · 20.", ans:"4700", sol:"5 · 47 · 20 = 47 · (5 · 20) = 47 · 100 = 4,700."},
    {q:"Three shelves hold 130 books. The first shelf has 40 books, the second has 15 more books than the first. How many books are on the third shelf?", ans:"35", sol:"1) 40 + 15 = 55 — on the second; 2) 40 + 55 = 95 — on the two shelves; 3) 130 − 95 = 35. As an expression: 130 − 40 − (40 + 15) = 35."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 5 · (x + 3) − 2x at x = 6.", ans:"33", sol:"5 · (6 + 3) − 2 · 6 = 5 · 9 − 12 = 45 − 12 = 33."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"A real receipt", b:"Take a receipt from a store (or a shopping list with prices from an ad flyer). Write the cost of the whole purchase as a single expression: for example, 2 · 65 + 3 · 48 + 120. Write out the order-of-operations plan, calculate it, and compare the result with the total on the receipt. Then figure out what expression would show the change from a 1,000-ruble bill."},
    {t:"A formula from life", b:"Measure the length and width of a notebook, a book, or a tabletop with a ruler, in centimeters. The perimeter of a rectangle is calculated with the expression 2 · (a + b), where a and b are the length and width. Substitute your measurements for the letters and find the perimeter, then check it: measure all four sides and add them up. Did it match? Do this for three objects."},
    {t:"The &ldquo;Guess the Number&rdquo; game", b:"Play with a friend or a parent. Let your friend think of a number and, without telling you what it is, do this chain: multiply it by 3, add 6, divide by 3, and subtract the number they thought of. You immediately announce the answer — it's always 2! Write the chain as a literal expression (3 · n + 6) : 3 − n, substitute a few numbers, and check it. Come up with your own chain with the same &ldquo;secret.&rdquo;"},
    {t:"A bar diagram from your daily activities", b:"Recall how many minutes today went to homework, the commute, play, and sleep, and make up a problem like: &ldquo;An evening has 180 minutes: homework takes 45 minutes, reading takes twice as long, and the rest is play. How many minutes of play are there?&rdquo; Draw a bar of 18 squares on grid paper (1 square = 10 minutes), divide it into parts, and solve the problem step by step, then with a single expression."},
    {t:"Quick calculation versus a calculator", b:"Take five problems for convenient calculation: 25 · 13 · 4, 47 + 88 + 53, 125 · 9 · 8, 64 · 9 + 64, 250 · 7 · 4. Run a race: one person calculates in their head using the properties of operations, the other uses a calculator. Time it. The winner is usually whoever figured out how to group the &ldquo;round&rdquo; numbers!"}
  ]
};

