CONTENT_EN.mot.steps = [
  {title:"Distance, speed, and time", body:
    "<p>Motion problems are some of the most common in math: something drives, walks, or sails, and we need to find out how far, how fast, or how long. The good news: the whole topic rests on one simple formula.</p>"
    +"<p><b>Speed</b> is the distance an object covers in one unit of time (in 1 hour, 1 minute, 1 second). If a bus travels at a speed of 50 km/h, that means it covers 50 km every hour. Then in 3 hours it will cover 50 · 3 = 150 km. This is how the main formula is born:</p>"
    +"<div class='figure'>__SVG_MOT_INTRO__</div>"},
  {title:"The main formula: s = v · t", body:
    "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Distance = speed · time, that is, <b>s = v · t</b>. From this: <b>v = s : t</b> (speed), <b>t = s : v</b> (time). The last two formulas are simply the &ldquo;inverse&rdquo; operations of multiplication.</div><p>Examples. A pedestrian walks 4 hours at 5 km/h: s = 5 · 4 = 20 km. A train covered 360 km in 6 hours: v = 360 : 6 = 60 km/h. A cyclist needs to cover 48 km at a speed of 12 km/h: t = 48 : 12 = 4 h.</p>",
    exIdx:[0]},
  {title:"Units of speed", body:
    "<p>The units vary: km/h, m/min, m/s. The main rule — <b>the units must match</b>: if the speed is in meters per minute, the time must also be taken in minutes, and the distance will come out in meters. If the units differ, convert them first.</p><p>How do you convert 72 km/h to m/s? In 1 hour a car covers 72 km = 72,000 m. There are 3600 seconds in an hour. So in 1 second: 72,000 : 3600 = 20 m. So, 72 km/h = 20 m/s. The other way: 15 m/s is 15 · 3600 = 54,000 m per hour, that is, 54 km/h.</p>",
    exIdx:[1]},
  {title:"Closing speed and separating speed", body:
    "<p>When two objects move at once, the main question is: how fast does <b>the distance between them change</b>? This quantity is called the <b>closing speed</b> (the distance shrinks) or the <b>separating speed</b> (the distance grows). It all depends on which way they're going.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_MOT_CASES__</div>"},
  {title:"Toward each other and in opposite directions", body:
    "<ul><li><b>Toward each other.</b> Each one &ldquo;eats up&rdquo; part of the distance, so the speeds <b>add up</b>: v closing = v₁ + v₂.</li><li><b>In opposite directions.</b> The distance grows from both sides, the speeds again <b>add up</b>: v separating = v₁ + v₂.</li></ul>",
    exIdx:[2,5]},
  {title:"Catching up and falling behind", body:
    "<ul><li><b>Catching up</b> (one catches up to the other, both go the same way). The one catching up is faster, so the distance shrinks only by the <b>difference</b> of the speeds: v closing = v₁ − v₂.</li><li><b>Falling behind</b> (same direction, but the one ahead is faster). The distance grows by the difference of the speeds: v separating = v₁ − v₂.</li></ul><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Mixing up when to add the speeds and when to subtract them. Check yourself with the question: &ldquo;are the objects moving toward each other or in different directions — or the same way?&rdquo; If the same way — subtract the speeds, otherwise — add them. Another mistake: dividing the distance by just one speed instead of the closing speed.</div>",
    exIdx:[3]},
  {title:"Time to meet and time to catch up", body:
    "<div class='figure'>__SVG_MOT_MEET_ANIM__</div><p>Since the closing speed is &ldquo;how many kilometers per hour disappear between the objects,&rdquo; the time until they meet is found by the same formula: <b>t = distance : closing speed</b>. For example, a hiker is 12 km ahead, walking at 4 km/h, and a cyclist catches up to him at 10 km/h. The closing speed is 10 − 4 = 6 km/h, the time to catch up: 12 : 6 = 2 h.</p><p>If one of them set out earlier, first work out how far he managed to travel (or how much of a &ldquo;lead&rdquo; he got) before the second one started. That becomes the new starting distance.</p>",
    exIdx:[4]},
  {title:"A motion graph", body:
    "<p>Motion can be drawn: along the horizontal axis we mark time t (in hours), along the vertical — distance s (in kilometers). An object moving at constant speed is shown as a <b>straight line</b>: the steeper the line, the greater the speed. Say two towns are 200 km apart, and cars set out toward each other: the first from the start (distance 0) at 40 km/h, the second from the point 200 km away at 60 km/h. The first line goes up, the second goes down.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_MOT_GRAPH__</div>"},
  {title:"Checking the meeting point on the graph", body:
    "<p>The point where the lines cross is the meeting point: both objects are in the same place at the same moment. Let's check by calculation: closing speed 40 + 60 = 100 km/h, time 200 : 100 = 2 h; by then the first car has covered 40 · 2 = 80 km. That's exactly what's labeled on the graph at the meeting point.</p>",
    exIdx:[6]}
];

/* ---------- logic ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.logic = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to work with sets and Euler circles, count possibilities without missing any, and solve simple logic problems about &ldquo;knights and liars.&rdquo;",
  explain: "<p>Math isn't just numbers. It also teaches you to carefully sort objects into groups, to count possibilities honestly, and to reason so that the conclusion can't be disputed. All three skills matter both in olympiads and in everyday life: for example, when you need to figure out how many different outfits you can put together from the clothes you have.</p>"
  + "<h3>Sets, elements, subsets</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>set</b> is a collection of objects gathered by some shared feature. The objects themselves are called <b>elements</b>. A set is written in curly braces: A = {2, 4, 6}. About the number 4 we say &ldquo;4 belongs to A&rdquo; and write 4 ∈ A, and about the number 5 — &ldquo;5 doesn't belong to A.&rdquo; The order of the elements doesn't matter, and repeats don't count: {1, 2, 2, 3} is the same as {1, 2, 3}, which has three elements.</p>"
  + "<p>A set with no elements at all is called <b>empty</b> and is written ∅. For example, the set of prime numbers divisible by 4 is empty: any number divisible by 4 is also divisible by 2 and by 4, so it can't be prime. If every element of set B is also in set A, then B is called a <b>subset</b> of A. Example: {2, 4} is a subset of the set {2, 4, 6}. Both the set itself and the empty set count as subsets.</p>"
  + "<p>The set {a, b, c} has eight subsets: ∅, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a, b}, {a, c}, {b, c}, {a, b, c}. In general, a set of n elements has exactly 2^n subsets: each element can be &ldquo;taken&rdquo; or &ldquo;left out,&rdquo; and that's true for every element.</p>"
  + "<h3>Intersection and union</h3>"
  + "<p>Let A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} and B = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8}. The <b>intersection</b> A ∩ B is the shared elements: {4, 5, 6}. The <b>union</b> A ∪ B is all the elements that are in at least one of the sets, with shared ones written only once: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}.</p>"
  + "<p>Let's count: A has six elements, B has five, together 6 + 5 = 11, but the union has only 8. We counted the three shared elements twice. So, 11 − 3 = 8.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The inclusion-exclusion formula for two sets: n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B). Here n(A) is the number of elements in A. We subtract the shared part because it ended up in the sum twice when we added.</div>"
  + "<h3>Euler circles</h3>"
  + "<p>To avoid getting confused, sets are drawn as circles, and everything being discussed as a rectangle. These drawings are called <b>Euler circles</b> (or Euler–Venn diagrams). The shared part of the circles — the intersection — is counted first.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_SET_EULER_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<p>Example from the diagram: there are 30 students in the class, 18 play soccer, 15 play chess, 7 do both. Anyone who does neither stands in the rectangle outside the circles.</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Adding 18 + 15 = 33 and being surprised that it's more than 30. The students who go to both clubs ended up in the sum twice. Always find &ldquo;both&rdquo; first and subtract them.</div>"
  + "<h3>Statements: &ldquo;all,&rdquo; &ldquo;some,&rdquo; &ldquo;none&rdquo;</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>statement</b> is a sentence about which you can say whether it's true or false. &ldquo;7 · 8 = 56&rdquo; is a true statement, &ldquo;7 · 8 = 54&rdquo; is false. The question &ldquo;What time is it?&rdquo; and the exclamation &ldquo;Hooray!&rdquo; are not statements.</p>"
  + "<ul><li><b>&ldquo;All,&rdquo; &ldquo;every&rdquo;</b> — a general claim. One <b>counterexample</b> is enough to disprove it: &ldquo;All prime numbers are odd&rdquo; is false, because 2 is prime and even.</li><li><b>&ldquo;Some,&rdquo; &ldquo;there exists&rdquo;</b> — means &ldquo;at least one.&rdquo; One example is enough to confirm it: &ldquo;Some prime numbers are even&rdquo; is true.</li><li><b>&ldquo;None&rdquo;</b> — no element fits. One fitting example disproves such a claim.</li></ul>"
  + "<p>The <b>negation</b> of a statement is a statement that's false exactly when the original is true, and vice versa. The negation of &ldquo;All the students came&rdquo; is &ldquo;Some students didn't come.&rdquo;</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>The negation of &ldquo;all&rdquo; is not &ldquo;none.&rdquo; To make &ldquo;All the students came&rdquo; false, it's enough for just one to be missing, not all of them. The correct negation: &ldquo;Not all came,&rdquo; that is, &ldquo;some didn't come.&rdquo;</div>"
  + "<h3>Enumerating possibilities, trees, and the product rule</h3>"
  + "<p>How many two-digit numbers can be written using the digits 1, 2, 3 if the digits don't repeat? We go through them in order. Starting with 1: 12, 13. Starting with 2: 21, 23. Starting with 3: 31, 32. 6 in total. Going through them in order — &ldquo;first all the numbers starting with 1, then all starting with 2&rdquo; — guarantees that we don't miss any or count any twice.</p>"
  + "<p>The same enumeration can be drawn as a <b>tree of possibilities</b>: three branches come out of the root (the first digit is 1, 2, or 3), and from each — two more branches (the second digit is either of the two remaining). The tree has 3 · 2 = 6 leaves.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The product rule: if the first choice can be made in m ways, and after it the second choice in n ways (for any first choice), then there are m · n pairs in total. For example, 3 T-shirts and 4 pairs of shorts give 3 · 4 = 12 outfits. For three choices, you multiply three numbers, and so on.</div>"
  + "<h3>Flowcharts: step-by-step logic</h3>"
  + "<p>A sequence of actions with a condition check is conveniently drawn as a flowchart. An oval is the start and end, a rectangle is an action, a diamond is a question with &ldquo;yes&rdquo; and &ldquo;no&rdquo; answers. Here's a program: input a number n; if n is even, divide it by 2, otherwise multiply it by 3 and add 1; output the result.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_FLOW__</div>"
  + "<p>Let's check it on numbers. n = 10: even, answer &ldquo;yes,&rdquo; we divide: 10 : 2 = 5. n = 7: not even, answer &ldquo;no,&rdquo; we calculate 7 · 3 + 1 = 22.</p>"
  + "<h3>Knights and liars</h3>"
  + "<p>On a special island live <b>knights</b>, who always tell the truth, and <b>liars</b>, who always lie. The task is to figure out who's who from the islanders' words. There's one method: assume the person is a knight, and check whether that leads to a contradiction; if it does, he's a liar.</p>"
  + "<p>For example, a person says: &ldquo;I am a liar.&rdquo; If he's a knight, then he told a lie — a contradiction. If he's a liar, then he told the truth — also a contradiction. So, no one on the island can ever say such a phrase.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Whatever a knight says is true; a liar only says things that are false. A liar's words &ldquo;We are both knights&rdquo; mean only that this is false, that is, at least one of the two is a liar.</div>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Write the set of two-digit numbers divisible by 25, and find the number of its elements.", s:"The two-digit numbers divisible by 25 are 25, 50, and 75 (100 is already three digits). The set is {25, 50, 75}, which has 3 elements."},
    {t:"Find the intersection and union of the sets D(12) and D(18) — the divisors of 12 and 18. How many elements are in the union?", s:"D(12) = {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12}, D(18) = {1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18}. The intersection — the shared divisors: {1, 2, 3, 6}. The union: {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18}, which has 8 elements. Check with the formula: 6 + 6 − 4 = 8."},
    {t:"There were 50 guests at a party. 32 drank tea, 27 drank coffee, and 12 guests drank both. How many guests drank neither tea nor coffee?", s:"At least one drink was had by 32 + 27 − 12 = 47 guests (those who drank both were subtracted once). Drank nothing: 50 − 47 = 3 guests."},
    {t:"How many two-digit numbers can be written using the digits 1, 2, 3 if the digits (a) can repeat; (b) can't repeat?", s:"A tree of possibilities. (a) First digit — 3 ways, second — also 3: 3 · 3 = 9 numbers (11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33). (b) First digit — 3 ways, second — either of the two remaining: 3 · 2 = 6 numbers (12, 13, 21, 23, 31, 32)."},
    {t:"Find the mistake. A student reasons: &ldquo;I checked the numbers 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13 — they're all prime and odd. So the claim &lsquo;All prime numbers are odd&rsquo; is proven.&rdquo;", s:"A general claim can't be proven by a few examples — even by a large number of them. But it can be disproved by a single counterexample: the number 2 is prime but even. So the claim is false."},
    {t:"Two islanders, A and B. A says: &ldquo;We are both liars.&rdquo; Which of them is a knight, and which is a liar?", s:"If A were a knight, he would have told the truth, meaning he'd be a liar — a contradiction. So A is a liar, and his words are false: &ldquo;both liars&rdquo; is wrong. One of them is a knight, and A is a liar, so the knight is B. Answer: A is a liar, B is a knight."},
    {t:"Using the flowchart: input n; if n is even, divide by 2, otherwise multiply by 3 and add 1. What do you get for n = 22? And if you apply the chart to the result once more?", s:"n = 22 is even, answer &ldquo;yes&rdquo;: 22 : 2 = 11. We apply the chart to 11: it's odd, answer &ldquo;no&rdquo;: 11 · 3 + 1 = 34. Result: 11, then 34."}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"The set A = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}. How many elements does it have?", ans:"6", hint:"Count the elements one by one, without skipping any.", sol:"Elements: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 — there are six."},
    {q:"Given the sets A = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11} and B = {3, 6, 9, 12}. How many elements are in their intersection A ∩ B?", ans:"2", hint:"The intersection is the elements that are in both A and B.", sol:"The shared elements: 3 and 9. So, A ∩ B = {3, 9}, which has 2 elements."},
    {q:"For the same sets A = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11} and B = {3, 6, 9, 12}, find the number of elements in the union A ∪ B.", ans:"8", hint:"Shared elements are written in the union only once. You can use the formula n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B).", sol:"A has six elements, B has four, two are shared (3 and 9). So the union has 6 + 4 − 2 = 8 elements: {1, 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 11, 12}."},
    {q:"There are 25 students in a class. 14 go to the drawing club, 9 go to the dance club, and 3 go to both clubs. How many students go to at least one of these clubs?", ans:"20", hint:"Those who go to both clubs got counted twice when adding.", sol:"14 + 9 − 3 = 20 students."},
    {q:"Petya has 3 T-shirts and 4 pairs of shorts. How many different &ldquo;T-shirt + shorts&rdquo; outfits can he put together?", ans:"12", hint:"With each T-shirt he can wear any of the four pairs of shorts. Use the product rule.", sol:"3 · 4 = 12 outfits."},
    {q:"How many true statements are there among these five: 1) 7 · 8 = 54; 2) 15 is divisible by 3; 3) 2^3 = 6; 4) the number 17 is prime; 5) 100 is greater than 99?", ans:"3", hint:"Check each statement separately.", sol:"1) 7 · 8 = 56, so the statement is false. 2) 15 = 3 · 5, true. 3) 2^3 = 8, false. 4) 17 is divisible only by 1 and 17, true. 5) 100 > 99, true. There are 3 true statements."},
    {q:"How many subsets in total does the set {a, b, c} have, counting the empty set and the set itself?", ans:"8", hint:"List the subsets in order: first with no elements, then with one, then with two, then with all three.", sol:"With no elements: ∅ — 1. With one: {a}, {b}, {c} — 3. With two: {a, b}, {a, c}, {b, c} — 3. With three: {a, b, c} — 1. Total 1 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 8 (this is 2^3)."},
    {q:"How many three-digit numbers can be written using the digits 1, 2, and 3, if each digit is used exactly once?", ans:"6", hint:"The first digit can be chosen in three ways. How many digits are left for the second place?", sol:"The first place — any of 3 digits, the second — any of the 2 remaining, the third — the last one left: 3 · 2 · 1 = 6. These are the numbers 123, 132, 213, 231, 312, 321."},
    {q:"There are 40 students in a class. 25 like math, 22 like Russian, and 12 like both subjects. How many students like neither math nor Russian?", ans:"5", hint:"First find how many students like at least one subject, without counting anyone twice.", sol:"At least one subject is liked by 25 + 22 − 12 = 35 people. Liking neither: 40 − 35 = 5."},
    {q:"There are 3 roads from city A to city B, 4 roads from B to C, and 2 roads from C to D. In how many ways can you travel from A to D through B and C?", ans:"24", hint:"Three choices in a row: multiply the number of ways for each leg.", sol:"By the product rule: 3 · 4 · 2 = 24 ways."},
    {q:"Which of these statements is the negation of the statement &ldquo;All the students in the class like chocolate&rdquo;?", opts:["No student in the class likes chocolate","Some students in the class don't like chocolate","All the students in the class don't like chocolate","Some students in the class like chocolate"], ans:1, hint:"The negation must be true exactly when the original statement is false. How many students need to dislike chocolate for &ldquo;all like it&rdquo; to become false?", sol:"The statement &ldquo;All like chocolate&rdquo; becomes false as soon as there's at least one student who doesn't like chocolate. That's exactly what &ldquo;Some students don't like chocolate&rdquo; means. The options &ldquo;No one likes it&rdquo; and &ldquo;All don't like it&rdquo; are too strong, while &ldquo;Some like it&rdquo; can be true together with the original."},
    {q:"Claim: &ldquo;All the numbers in the list 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 are odd.&rdquo; How many numbers in this list are counterexamples to this claim?", ans:"2", hint:"A counterexample is a number for which the claim is false, that is, an even number.", sol:"The even numbers in the list: 6 and 12. Each of them disproves the claim, so there are 2 counterexamples."},
    {q:"Three islanders of knights and liars — A, B, and C. A says: &ldquo;All three of us are liars.&rdquo; B says: &ldquo;Exactly one of us is a knight.&rdquo; C stays silent. How many knights are among them?", ans:"1", hint:"First work out whether A could be a knight. Then look at what that would mean for A's statement.", sol:"If A were a knight, then his words &ldquo;we're all liars&rdquo; would be true, making him a liar — a contradiction. So A is a liar, and at least one of the three is a knight. If B is a liar, then only C is a knight, and then &ldquo;exactly one knight&rdquo; would be true — but B is a liar, a contradiction. So B is a knight, and exactly one is a knight: it's B. There is 1 knight."},
    {q:"There's a group of 28 tourists, and each one knows at least one of two languages — English or German. 20 people know English, 15 know German. How many tourists know both languages?", ans:"7", hint:"The formula n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B): here everything is known except the intersection.", sol:"All 28 people know at least one language. From the formula: 28 = 20 + 15 − x, so x = 35 − 28 = 7. 7 tourists know both languages."},
    {q:"How many three-digit even numbers can be written using the digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, if the digits in the number don't repeat?", ans:"24", hint:"Start with the last digit: it must be even. Then choose the first two.", sol:"The last digit is even: 2 or 4 — 2 ways. The first digit is any of the 4 remaining, the second is any of the 3 remaining. By the product rule: 2 · 4 · 3 = 24 numbers."},
    {q:"How many natural numbers from 1 to 100 are divisible by 2 or by 5 (or by both)?", ans:"60", hint:"Add how many numbers are divisible by 2 and how many by 5, then subtract the numbers that were counted twice: those divisible by 10.", sol:"Divisible by 2: 50 numbers, by 5: 20 numbers, by 10 (that is, by both): 10 numbers. By the formula: 50 + 20 − 10 = 60."},
    {q:"Six friends met, and each one shook hands with every other one exactly once. How many handshakes were there in total?", ans:"15", hint:"The first friend shakes hands with the other five. How many new handshakes does the second one add, if he already greeted the first?", sol:"The first shakes hands with 5 friends, the second with 4 more new ones, the third with 3, the fourth with 2, the fifth with 1, the sixth with none new. Total 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15 handshakes."},
    {q:"Three islanders of knights and liars. The first said: &ldquo;Exactly one of us is a liar.&rdquo; The second: &ldquo;Exactly two of us are liars.&rdquo; The third: &ldquo;Exactly three of us are liars.&rdquo; How many liars are among them?", ans:"2", hint:"The three statements can't all be true at the same time — they contradict each other. How many of them can be true?", sol:"The statements are different, so at most one can be true. Then there's one knight (the one who told the truth), and two liars. Let's check: the true statement should be &ldquo;exactly two liars&rdquo; — that's the second one's. So the second is a knight, the first and third are liars, and there really are two liars. Answer: 2."},
    {q:"A card has five statements written on it. 1: &ldquo;Exactly one statement on this card is false.&rdquo; 2: &ldquo;…exactly two are false.&rdquo; 3: &ldquo;…exactly three are false.&rdquo; 4: &ldquo;…exactly four are false.&rdquo; 5: &ldquo;…exactly five are false.&rdquo; How many statements on the card are false?", ans:"4", hint:"Two different statements can't both be true at the same time. So how many true statements are on the card? How many false ones, then?", sol:"The statements contradict each other, so at most one can be true. If none are true, then 5 are false, and statement 5 would turn out true — a contradiction. So exactly one is true, and 4 are false. This checks out: statement 4, &ldquo;exactly four false,&rdquo; is the true one. Answer: 4."},
    {q:"Among 100 tourists, 75 know English, 83 know German, and 10 know neither of these languages. How many tourists know both languages?", ans:"68", hint:"First find how many tourists know at least one of the two languages.", sol:"At least one language is known by 100 − 10 = 90 people. From the formula 90 = 75 + 83 − x we get x = 158 − 90 = 68. 68 tourists know both languages."},
    {q:"Given the sets A = {2, 5, 8, 11, 14} and B = {5, 10, 14, 20}. How many elements are in the union A ∪ B?", ans:"7", hint:"Shared elements are written in the union only once. You can use the formula n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B).", sol:"A has five elements, B has four, the shared elements 5 and 14 make two. By the formula: 5 + 4 − 2 = 7 elements: {2, 5, 8, 10, 11, 14, 20}."},
    {q:"Katya has 4 skirts and 5 blouses. How many different &ldquo;skirt + blouse&rdquo; outfits can she put together?", ans:"20", hint:"With each skirt she can wear any of the blouses. Use the product rule.", sol:"4 · 5 = 20 outfits."},
    {q:"Three islanders live on an island: P, K, and R — each is either a knight (always tells the truth) or a liar (always lies). P says: &ldquo;K is a liar.&rdquo; K says: &ldquo;R is a liar.&rdquo; R says: &ldquo;P and K are both liars.&rdquo; How many knights are among P, K, and R?", ans:"1", hint:"Work through the cases in a chain: if P is a knight, then K is a liar; then K's words about R must be false, so R is a knight. Check what R would then say, and whether a contradiction arises.", sol:"If P is a knight, then K is a liar (by P's words), so K's words &ldquo;R is a liar&rdquo; are false, meaning R is a knight. But then R's words &ldquo;P and K are both liars&rdquo; must be true, while P is a knight — a contradiction. So P is a liar. Then P's words are false, meaning K is a knight. K's words &ldquo;R is a liar&rdquo; are true (K is a knight), so R is a liar. Let's check R's words: &ldquo;P and K are both liars&rdquo; — false (K is a knight), and that's correct, since R is a liar. Everything checks out: P is a liar, K is a knight, R is a liar. There is only one knight."},
    {q:"50 people took part in a survey. 32 of them like tea, 28 like coffee, and 15 like both. How many people like neither tea nor coffee?", ans:"5", hint:"First find how many people like at least one of the drinks, without counting anyone twice.", sol:"At least one drink is liked by 32 + 28 − 15 = 45 people. Liking neither: 50 − 45 = 5 people."},
    {q:"There are 30 students in a class. 18 do swimming, 12 do soccer, and 5 people do both. How many students do only swimming?", ans:"13", hint:"Draw Euler circles and start with the shared part.", sol:"Of the 18 swimmers, 5 also play soccer. Only swimming is done by 18 − 5 = 13 students. Answer: 13."},
    {q:"How many numbers from 1 to 50 are divisible by neither 2 nor 3?", ans:"17", hint:"First find how many numbers are divisible by 2 or by 3, without counting those divisible by 6 twice.", sol:"Divisible by 2: 25 numbers, by 3: 16 (3, 6, …, 48), by 6: 8 (6, 12, …, 48). Divisible by 2 or 3: 25 + 16 − 8 = 33 numbers. Divisible by neither 2 nor 3: 50 − 33 = 17. Answer: 17."},
    {q:"How many two-digit numbers can be written using the digits 0, 1, 2, 3, if the digits in the number can repeat?", ans:"12", hint:"Think about which digit can't be in the first place of a two-digit number.", sol:"The first digit is 1, 2, or 3 (a number can't start with zero): 3 ways. The second is any of the four: 4 ways. Total 3 · 4 = 12 numbers. Answer: 12."},
    {q:"The cafeteria has 3 soups for lunch (one of them is mushroom), 4 main dishes, and 2 drinks. A lunch consists of a soup, a main dish, and a drink. Katya doesn't eat mushroom soup. How many different lunches can Katya choose?", ans:"16", hint:"First count how many soups Katya is actually choosing from. Then use the product rule.", sol:"Katya's soups: 3 − 1 = 2. Lunches: 2 · 4 · 2 = 16. Answer: 16."},
    {q:"On an island of knights (who always tell the truth) and liars (who always lie), two islanders meet: A and B. A said: &ldquo;At least one of the two of us is a liar.&rdquo; How many knights are there among A and B?", ans:"1", hint:"Check whether A could be a liar: what would his statement mean then?", sol:"If A is a liar, then his words are false, meaning there are no liars among them — but A himself is a liar, a contradiction. So A is a knight, and his words are true: there is at least one liar. That's B. There is 1 knight. Answer: 1."},
    {q:"There are 5 teams in a soccer tournament. Each team played two matches against every other team: one at home and one away. How many matches were played in total?", ans:"20", hint:"First find how many different pairs of teams can be made, then account for each pair playing twice.", sol:"Pairs of teams: the first plays 4 others, the second plays 3 more new ones, then 2, then 1: 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10 pairs. Each pair played 2 matches: 10 · 2 = 20. Answer: 20."},
    {q:"How many subsets of the set {1, 2, 3, 4} contain the number 1?", ans:"8", hint:"The number 1 must be included. For each of the other three numbers, decide: take it or not.", sol:"The element 1 is always included, and for each of the elements 2, 3, 4 there are 2 options: take it or not. Total 2 · 2 · 2 = 8 subsets: {1}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {1, 4}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 2, 4}, {1, 3, 4}, {1, 2, 3, 4}. Answer: 8."},
    {q:"It's known that all the straight-A students in the class go to the chess club. Which statement must be true?", opts:["Everyone who goes to the chess club is a straight-A student","If a student doesn't go to the chess club, then he isn't a straight-A student","All the students in the class go to the chess club","No straight-A student goes to the chess club"], ans:1, hint:"Draw Euler circles: where is the &ldquo;straight-A students&rdquo; circle relative to the &ldquo;chess club&rdquo; circle?", sol:"The &ldquo;straight-A students&rdquo; circle lies entirely inside the &ldquo;chess club&rdquo; circle. So a student outside the &ldquo;chess club&rdquo; circle can't be inside the &ldquo;straight-A students&rdquo; circle — he isn't a straight-A student. But non-straight-A students can also go to the club, and not every student in the class has to go to the club. The second statement is correct."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"In a class, every student studies at least one of two languages: English or German. 25 people study English, 20 study German. It's known that exactly 33 students study only one language. How many students are in the class in total?", ans:"39", hint:"Let x people study both languages. How many then study only English and how many only German?", sol:"Let x people study both languages. Only English is studied by 25 − x, only German by 20 − x. Total &ldquo;only one language&rdquo;: (25 − x) + (20 − x) = 45 − 2x = 33, so x = 6. Total students: 25 + 20 − 6 = 39. Check: only English 19, only German 14, 19 + 14 = 33, and 19 + 14 + 6 = 39."},
  test: [
    {q:"Given the sets A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} and B = {2, 4, 6, 8}. How many elements are in the intersection A ∩ B?", ans:"3", sol:"Shared elements: 2, 4, 6. There are three."},
    {q:"For the same sets A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} and B = {2, 4, 6, 8}, find the number of elements in the union A ∪ B.", ans:"7", sol:"6 + 4 − 3 = 7: these are the elements 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8."},
    {q:"How many subsets in total does the set {p, q} have, counting the empty set and the set itself?", ans:"4", sol:"∅, {p}, {q}, {p, q} — 4 subsets in total (this is 2^2)."},
    {q:"There are 36 students in a class. 20 do volleyball, 17 do swimming, and 9 do both. How many students do neither volleyball nor swimming?", ans:"8", sol:"At least one sport is done by 20 + 17 − 9 = 28 people. Doing neither: 36 − 28 = 8."},
    {q:"The cafeteria has 4 kinds of soup, 5 main dishes, and 3 drinks. How many different lunches made of a soup, a main dish, and a drink can be put together?", ans:"60", sol:"By the product rule: 4 · 5 · 3 = 60."},
    {q:"How many true statements are there among these four: 1) the number 1 is prime; 2) some prime numbers are even; 3) all numbers divisible by 4 are divisible by 2; 4) no number divisible by 5 ends in the digit 5?", ans:"2", sol:"1) False: a prime number has exactly two divisors, while 1 has only one. 2) True: the number 2. 3) True: a number divisible by 4 is also divisible by 2. 4) False: the number 5 is divisible by 5 and ends in 5. There are 2 true statements."},
    {q:"Two islanders of knights and liars. A says: &ldquo;We are both knights.&rdquo; B says: &ldquo;A is a liar.&rdquo; How many knights are among them?", ans:"1", sol:"If A is a knight, then both are knights, and then B's statement &ldquo;A is a liar&rdquo; would be true — a contradiction. So A is a liar. Then B's words &ldquo;A is a liar&rdquo; are true, and B is a knight. There is one knight."},
    {q:"How many three-digit numbers can be written using the digits 0, 1, 2, 3, if the digits in the number don't repeat?", ans:"18", sol:"The first digit can't be 0: 3 ways (1, 2, or 3). The second is any of the 3 remaining (including 0), the third is any of the 2 remaining. 3 · 3 · 2 = 18."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"Euler circles on your class (or your family)", b:"Ask 10–15 people you know (classmates, family, friends) two questions: &ldquo;Do you like apples?&rdquo; and &ldquo;Do you like bananas?&rdquo; Draw two overlapping circles inside a rectangle and write down, in each of the four regions, how many people ended up there. Check with the formula: the sum of the numbers from the two circles minus the shared part plus those who ended up outside the circles should equal the number of people surveyed."},
    {t:"How many outfits are in your closet?", b:"Count how many T-shirts (or shirts) you have and how many pairs of pants (or shorts). Use the product rule to find how many different outfits can be made. Then add a third thing: sneakers or boots — and recount. Pick any three outfits and put them on to make sure you're not counting the same thing twice."},
    {t:"A coin-flip tree", b:"Take a coin and flip it three times in a row, writing down the result each time (H — heads, T — tails). Draw a tree of possibilities: two branches out of the root, two more out of each, and so on. How many leaves did you end up with in total? Check with the product rule: 2 · 2 · 2. Write out all the outcomes and find how many of them have heads coming up exactly twice."},
    {t:"The &ldquo;Knights and Liars&rdquo; game for two", b:"Take a deck of cards or two coins. One player secretly decides whether he's a knight or a liar, and makes a statement about himself and his friend: &ldquo;We are both knights,&rdquo; &ldquo;At least one of us is a liar,&rdquo; or &ldquo;I'm a knight and you're a liar.&rdquo; The second player must figure out who's who from the statement. Swap roles. Write down which statements let you identify both, and which only one."},
    {t:"Test the flowchart on numbers", b:"Take a sheet of grid paper and draw a flowchart: input a number n; if n is even, divide by 2, otherwise multiply by 3 and add 1. Then run it for the numbers 6, 7, 10, and 15. See what happens if you feed the result back into the chart again. For example, starting from 6, you'll get the chain 6, 3, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 — continue it and write down what happens next."}
  ]
};

