CONTENT_EN.logic.steps = [
  {title:"What is a set", body:
    "<p>Math isn't just numbers. It also teaches you to carefully sort objects into groups, to count possibilities honestly, and to reason so that the conclusion can't be disputed. All three skills matter both in olympiads and in everyday life: for example, when you need to figure out how many different outfits you can put together from the clothes you have.</p><p><b>A set</b> is a collection of objects gathered by some shared feature. The objects themselves are called <b>elements</b>. A set is written in curly braces: A = {2, 4, 6}. About the number 4 we say &ldquo;4 belongs to A&rdquo; and write 4 ∈ A, and about the number 5 — &ldquo;5 doesn't belong to A.&rdquo; The order of the elements doesn't matter, and repeats don't count: {1, 2, 2, 3} is the same as {1, 2, 3}, which has three elements.</p>",
    exIdx:[0]},
  {title:"The empty set and subsets", body:
    "<p>A set with no elements at all is called <b>empty</b> and is written ∅. For example, the set of prime numbers divisible by 4 is empty: any number divisible by 4 is also divisible by 2 and by 4, so it can't be prime. If every element of set B is also in set A, then B is called a <b>subset</b> of A. Example: {2, 4} is a subset of the set {2, 4, 6}. Both the set itself and the empty set count as subsets.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_SUBSET_VENN__</div>"},
  {title:"How many subsets in total", body:
    "<p>The set {a, b, c} has eight subsets: ∅, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a, b}, {a, c}, {b, c}, {a, b, c}. In general, a set of n elements has exactly 2^n subsets: each element can be &ldquo;taken&rdquo; or &ldquo;left out,&rdquo; and that's true for every element.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_SUBSET_COUNT__</div>"},
  {title:"Intersection and union", body:
    "<p>Let A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} and B = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8}. The <b>intersection</b> A ∩ B is the shared elements: {4, 5, 6}. The <b>union</b> A ∪ B is all the elements that are in at least one of the sets, with shared ones written only once: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}.</p><p>Let's count: A has six elements, B has five, together 6 + 5 = 11, but the union has only 8. We counted the three shared elements twice. So, 11 − 3 = 8.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_UNION_INTERSECT__</div>"},
  {title:"The inclusion-exclusion formula", body:
    "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The inclusion-exclusion formula for two sets: n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B). Here n(A) is the number of elements in A. We subtract the shared part because it ended up in the sum twice when we added.</div>",
    exIdx:[1]},
  {title:"Euler circles", body:
    "<p>To avoid getting confused, sets are drawn as circles, and everything being discussed as a rectangle. These drawings are called <b>Euler circles</b> (or Euler–Venn diagrams). The shared part of the circles — the intersection — is counted first.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_SET_EULER_ANIM__</div><p>Example from the diagram: there are 30 students in the class, 18 play soccer, 15 play chess, 7 do both. Anyone who does neither stands in the rectangle outside the circles.</p><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Adding 18 + 15 = 33 and being surprised that it's more than 30. The students who go to both clubs ended up in the sum twice. Always find &ldquo;both&rdquo; first and subtract them.</div>",
    exIdx:[2]},
  {title:"Statements: all, some, none", body:
    "<p>A <b>statement</b> is a sentence about which you can say whether it's true or false. &ldquo;7 · 8 = 56&rdquo; is a true statement, &ldquo;7 · 8 = 54&rdquo; is false. The question &ldquo;What time is it?&rdquo; and the exclamation &ldquo;Hooray!&rdquo; are not statements.</p><ul><li><b>&ldquo;All,&rdquo; &ldquo;every&rdquo;</b> — a general claim. One <b>counterexample</b> is enough to disprove it: &ldquo;All prime numbers are odd&rdquo; is false, because 2 is prime and even.</li><li><b>&ldquo;Some,&rdquo; &ldquo;there exists&rdquo;</b> — means &ldquo;at least one.&rdquo; One example is enough to confirm it: &ldquo;Some prime numbers are even&rdquo; is true.</li><li><b>&ldquo;None&rdquo;</b> — no element fits. One fitting example disproves such a claim.</li></ul><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_QUANTIFIERS__</div>"},
  {title:"Negating a statement", body:
    "<p>The <b>negation</b> of a statement is a statement that's false exactly when the original is true, and vice versa. The negation of &ldquo;All the students came&rdquo; is &ldquo;Some students didn't come.&rdquo;</p><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>The negation of &ldquo;all&rdquo; is not &ldquo;none.&rdquo; To make &ldquo;All the students came&rdquo; false, it's enough for just one to be missing, not all of them. The correct negation: &ldquo;Not all came,&rdquo; that is, &ldquo;some didn't come.&rdquo;</div>",
    exIdx:[4]},
  {title:"Enumerating possibilities", body:
    "<p>How many two-digit numbers can be written using the digits 1, 2, 3 if the digits don't repeat? We go through them in order. Starting with 1: 12, 13. Starting with 2: 21, 23. Starting with 3: 31, 32. 6 in total. Going through them in order — &ldquo;first all the numbers starting with 1, then all starting with 2&rdquo; — guarantees that we don't miss any or count any twice.</p>",
    exIdx:[3]},
  {title:"The tree of possibilities and the product rule", body:
    "<p>The same enumeration can be drawn as a <b>tree of possibilities</b>: three branches come out of the root (the first digit is 1, 2, or 3), and from each — two more branches (the second digit is either of the two remaining). The tree has 3 · 2 = 6 leaves.</p><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The product rule: if the first choice can be made in m ways, and after it the second choice in n ways (for any first choice), then there are m · n pairs in total. For example, 3 T-shirts and 4 pairs of shorts give 3 · 4 = 12 outfits. For three choices, you multiply three numbers, and so on.</div><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_PRODUCT_RULE__</div>"},
  {title:"Flowcharts: step-by-step logic", body:
    "<p>A sequence of actions with a condition check is conveniently drawn as a flowchart. An oval is the start and end, a rectangle is an action, a diamond is a question with &ldquo;yes&rdquo; and &ldquo;no&rdquo; answers. Here's a program: input a number n; if n is even, divide it by 2, otherwise multiply it by 3 and add 1; output the result.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_LOGIC_FLOW__</div><p>Let's check it on numbers. n = 10: even, answer &ldquo;yes,&rdquo; we divide: 10 : 2 = 5. n = 7: not even, answer &ldquo;no,&rdquo; we calculate 7 · 3 + 1 = 22.</p>",
    exIdx:[6]},
  {title:"Knights and liars", body:
    "<p>On a special island live <b>knights</b>, who always tell the truth, and <b>liars</b>, who always lie. The task is to figure out who's who from the islanders' words. There's one method: assume the person is a knight, and check whether that leads to a contradiction; if it does, he's a liar.</p><p>For example, a person says: &ldquo;I am a liar.&rdquo; If he's a knight, then he told a lie — a contradiction. If he's a liar, then he told the truth — also a contradiction. So, no one on the island can ever say such a phrase.</p><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Whatever a knight says is true; a liar only says things that are false. A liar's words &ldquo;We are both knights&rdquo; mean only that this is false, that is, at least one of the two is a liar.</div>",
    exIdx:[5]}
];

/* ---------- frac ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.frac = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll confidently reduce any fraction, add fractions with different denominators, and no longer get confused with multiplication and division.",
  explain: "<p>A fraction {a|b} shows this: the whole was divided into <b>b</b> equal parts, and <b>a</b> of them were taken. The number below the line is the <b>denominator</b>, the number above the line is the <b>numerator</b>.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_PIE__</div>"
  + "<h3>The basic property of a fraction</h3>"
  + "<p>If the numerator and denominator are multiplied (or divided) by the same number that isn't zero, the fraction doesn't change: {1|2} = {2|4} = {5|10}. Dividing by a common divisor is called <b>reducing</b>: {18|24} = {3|4} (divided by 6).</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_STRIPS__</div>"
  + "<p>Look at the &ldquo;wall of fractions&rdquo;: all the strips are the same length. The dashed line in the middle shows that {1|2}, {2|4}, {3|6}, and {6|12} cover the same part of the strip, that is, {1|2} = {2|4} = {3|6} = {6|12}. So a fraction can be written in different ways while its value stays the same.</p>"
  + "<h3>Four operations</h3>"
  + "<ul><li><b>Addition and subtraction.</b> First find a common denominator, then do the operation with the numerators: {1|2} + {1|3} = {3|6} + {2|6} = {5|6}.</li><li><b>Multiplication.</b> Numerator times numerator, denominator times denominator: {2|3} · {3|4} = {6|12} = {1|2}.</li><li><b>Division.</b> We multiply by the &ldquo;flipped&rdquo; fraction: {2|3} : {4|5} = {2|3} · {5|4} = {10|12} = {5|6}.</li></ul>"
  + "<p>Watch how it works:</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_ADD_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<h3>Mixed numbers</h3>"
  + "<p>1{3|4} is 1 + {3|4}. To convert to an improper fraction: (1 · 4 + 3) / 4 = {7|4}. Before multiplying and dividing, we always convert mixed numbers into improper fractions.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_NUMLINE__</div>"
  + "<p>Fractions have their place on the number line too: {1|2} sits exactly halfway between 0 and 1, while 1{3|4} sits between 1 and 2, closer to 2. In the diagram for 1{3|4}, you can see two parts: the whole-number part is 1, the fractional part is {3|4}, that is, 1{3|4} = 1 + {3|4} = {7|4}. The point 1{1|4} is one tick mark to the right of 1.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>You can only add and subtract fractions that have the same denominator.</div>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>{1|2} + {1|3} = {2|5} — you can't do it this way. A common-sense check: {2|5} is less than {1|2}, but a sum can't be smaller than one of its addends.</div>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Reduce the fraction {18|24}.", s:"We look for the greatest common divisor: GCD(18, 24) = 6. We divide the numerator and denominator by 6: {18|24} = {3|4}."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression {3|4} + {5|6}.", s:"The common denominator is 12. {3|4} = {9|12}, {5|6} = {10|12}. The sum: {19|12} = 1{7|12}."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression {2|5} : {4|15}.", s:"We replace division with multiplication: {2|5} · {15|4}. We reduce 15 and 5 by 5, and 2 and 4 by 2: {1|1} · {3|2} = {3|2} = 1{1|2}."},
    {t:"The school cafeteria has 2{1|3} vats of compote left. A portion of 1{5|6} vats was poured out for the students. How much compote is left?", s:"Let's convert to improper fractions: 2{1|3} = {7|3}, 1{5|6} = {11|6}. Let's bring them to a denominator of 6: {7|3} = {14|6}. Subtract: {14|6} − {11|6} = {3|6} = {1|2}. There's {1|2} of a vat left."},
    {t:"Find the mistake. A student wrote: {2|3} + {3|4} = {5|7}. Why is this wrong? Find the correct value of the expression.", s:"The mistake: you can't add numerators and denominators separately. A common-sense check: both {2|3} and {3|4} are more than a half, so their sum is more than 1, but {5|7} is less than 1. Correct: common denominator 12, {8|12} + {9|12} = {17|12} = 1{5|12}."},
    {t:"Compare the fractions {7|9} and {5|6} without bringing them to a common denominator.", s:"Let's see how much each fraction is short of a whole: 1 − {7|9} = {2|9}, 1 − {5|6} = {1|6}. Let's compare the missing parts: {2|9} is more than {1|6} (about 0.22 and 0.17). So {7|9} is farther from 1, that is, {7|9} < {5|6}. Check: {14|18} < {15|18}."},
    {t:"Find the missing denominator: {3|□} + {1|6} = {2|3}.", s:"Let's find the addend: {3|□} = {2|3} − {1|6} = {4|6} − {1|6} = {3|6}. So □ = 6. Check: {3|6} + {1|6} = {4|6} = {2|3}."},
    {t:"A rope is {3|4} m long. {2|5} of its length was cut off. How many meters of rope are left?", s:"They cut off {2|5} of {3|4}: {3|4} · {2|5} = {6|20} = {3|10} m. What's left: {3|4} − {3|10} = {15|20} − {6|20} = {9|20} m. Another way: what's left is 1 − {2|5} = {3|5} of the length, that is, {3|4} · {3|5} = {9|20} m."},
    {t:"The whole route is shown as a bar divided into 7 equal parts. A hiker has covered 4 parts, and has 12 km left to go. How many kilometers is the whole route?", s:"There are 7 − 4 = 3 parts left, and they equal 12 km. One part: 12 : 3 = 4 km. The whole route: 4 · 7 = 28 km. Check: covered 4 · 4 = 16 km, 12 km left, 28 km total."}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Reduce the fraction {12|18}. The answer should be an irreducible fraction.", ans:"2/3", reduced:true, hint:"Find the largest number that both 12 and 18 are divisible by.", sol:"GCD(12, 18) = 6, so {12|18} = {2|3}."},
    {q:"Reduce the fraction {35|45}.", ans:"7/9", reduced:true, hint:"Both 35 and 45 are divisible by 5.", sol:"{35|45} = {7|9}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {1|4} + {2|3}.", ans:"11/12", reduced:true, hint:"The common denominator is 12.", sol:"{3|12} + {8|12} = {11|12}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {5|6} − {1|4}.", ans:"7/12", reduced:true, hint:"The common denominator is 12: {5|6} = {10|12}.", sol:"{10|12} − {3|12} = {7|12}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {3|8} · {4|9}.", ans:"1/6", reduced:true, hint:"You can reduce 3 and 9, and 4 and 8, first.", sol:"{3·4|8·9} = {12|72} = {1|6}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {5|6} : {10|9}.", ans:"3/4", reduced:true, hint:"Division is multiplication by the flipped fraction {9|10}.", sol:"{5|6} · {9|10} = {45|60} = {3|4}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {2|3} + {3|5} − {1|15}.", ans:"6/5", reduced:true, hint:"The common denominator is 15. The answer may be an improper fraction.", sol:"{10|15} + {9|15} − {1|15} = {18|15} = {6|5}."},
    {q:"Find {3|5} of 40.", ans:"24", hint:"To find a fraction of a number, multiply the number by that fraction.", sol:"40 · {3|5} = 40 : 5 · 3 = 24."},
    {q:"Find the number, {2|7} of which equals 14.", ans:"49", hint:"If the part is known, the number is found by division: 14 : {2|7}.", sol:"14 : {2|7} = 14 · {7|2} = 49."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 2{1|2} · 1{1|5}.", ans:"3", hint:"Convert to improper fractions: {5|2} and {6|5}.", sol:"{5|2} · {6|5} = {30|10} = 3."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 1{2|3} + 2{3|4}.", ans:"4 5/12", reduced:true, hint:"Add the whole-number parts separately and the fractional parts separately, bringing them to a common denominator of 12.", sol:"Whole numbers: 1 + 2 = 3. Fractions: {2|3} + {3|4} = {8|12} + {9|12} = {17|12} = 1{5|12}. Total: 3 + 1{5|12} = 4{5|12}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 3{1|4} − 1{5|6}.", ans:"1 5/12", reduced:true, hint:"Convert both numbers into improper fractions with a denominator of 12: {13|4} and {11|6}.", sol:"3{1|4} = {13|4} = {39|12}, 1{5|6} = {11|6} = {22|12}. {39|12} − {22|12} = {17|12} = 1{5|12}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {2|3} : 1{1|3} · {4|5}.", ans:"2/5", reduced:true, hint:"First convert 1{1|3} into the improper fraction {4|3}, then do the operations in order, left to right.", sol:"{2|3} : {4|3} = {2|3} · {3|4} = {1|2}. Then {1|2} · {4|5} = {4|10} = {2|5}."},
    {q:"On the first day, a hiker covered {3|8} of the whole route, and on the second day — {1|4} of the route more than on the first. What fraction of the route did the hiker cover over both days?", ans:"1", hint:"First find what fraction the hiker covered on the second day, then add it to the first day.", sol:"Second day: {3|8} + {1|4} = {3|8} + {2|8} = {5|8}. Total: {3|8} + {5|8} = {8|8} = 1, that is, the whole route."},
    {q:"Two pumps are filling a tank with water. The first fills {1|4} of the tank per hour, the second fills {1|6} of the tank per hour. What fraction of the tank will they fill together in 2 hours?", ans:"5/6", reduced:true, hint:"First find what fraction of the tank the pumps fill together in 1 hour, then multiply by 2.", sol:"Together in an hour: {1|4} + {1|6} = {3|12} + {2|12} = {5|12}. In 2 hours: {5|12} · 2 = {10|12} = {5|6}."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression: ({3|4} + {1|8}) · 2{2|3} − {1|2}.", ans:"1 5/6", reduced:true, hint:"First do the operation in parentheses, then multiply by the improper fraction 2{2|3} = {8|3}, and subtract at the end.", sol:"{3|4} + {1|8} = {6|8} + {1|8} = {7|8}. Multiply: {7|8} · {8|3} = {56|24} = {7|3}. Subtract: {7|3} − {1|2} = {14|6} − {3|6} = {11|6} = 1{5|6}."},
    {q:"In a library, {2|5} of all the books are fiction, {1|3} of all the books are textbooks, and the rest are reference books. What fraction of the books are reference books?", ans:"4/15", reduced:true, hint:"Add the known fractions and subtract from the whole (from 1).", sol:"{2|5} + {1|3} = {6|15} + {5|15} = {11|15}. Reference books: 1 − {11|15} = {4|15}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 2{1|3} − {5|6} : 1{1|4}.", ans:"5/3", reduced:true, hint:"By the order of operations, division comes first: {5|6} : 1{1|4}, then subtraction.", sol:"1{1|4} = {5|4}. {5|6} : {5|4} = {5|6} · {4|5} = {20|30} = {2|3}. Then 2{1|3} − {2|3} = {7|3} − {2|3} = {5|3}."},
    {q:"A piece of fabric 12{3|4} m long was cut into pieces of {3|4} m each. How many pieces were there?", ans:"17", hint:"Convert the length of the piece into an improper fraction and divide by {3|4}.", sol:"12{3|4} = {51|4}. {51|4} : {3|4} = {51|4} · {4|3} = {204|12} = 17."},
    {q:"A cyclist rode {2|5} of the whole route in the first hour, {1|3} of the remaining route in the second hour, and the remaining 24 km in the third hour. Find the length of the whole route.", ans:"60", hint:"First find out what fraction of the route was left after the first hour, then what fraction of that was covered in the second hour, and what was left for the third.", sol:"After the first hour, 1 − {2|5} = {3|5} of the route was left. In the second hour, they covered {1|3} of {3|5}, that is, {1|5} of the whole route. Left after the second hour: {3|5} − {1|5} = {2|5} of the route — and that's the third hour, that is, 24 km. So {2|5} of the route = 24 km, the whole route = 24 : 2 · 5 = 60 km."},
    {q:"Find the largest of the fractions {7|12}, {5|8}, {3|5}, {11|20} and write it as your answer.", ans:"5/8", reduced:true, hint:"Bring all the fractions to a common denominator of 120.", sol:"With denominator 120: {7|12} = {70|120}, {5|8} = {75|120}, {3|5} = {72|120}, {11|20} = {66|120}. The largest is {75|120} = {5|8}."},
    {q:"Find the numerator x for which the equality {5|6} − {x|12} = {1|4} is true.", ans:"7", hint:"Bring everything to a denominator of 12: {5|6} = {10|12}, {1|4} = {3|12}.", sol:"{10|12} − {x|12} = {3|12}, so x = 10 − 3 = 7. Check: {10|12} − {7|12} = {3|12} = {1|4}."},
    {q:"A student multiplied mixed numbers like this: 2{1|2} · 1{1|3} = 2 · 1 + {1|2} · {1|3} = 2{1|6}. Find the correct value of the product.", ans:"3 1/3", reduced:true, hint:"Before multiplying, mixed numbers need to be converted into improper fractions.", sol:"2{1|2} = {5|2}, 1{1|3} = {4|3}. {5|2} · {4|3} = {20|6} = {10|3} = 3{1|3}. The student's mistake is that you can't multiply the whole-number and fractional parts separately."},
    {q:"A camp has 96 children. The youngest make up {3|8} of all the children, {1|3} of the rest are the middle group, and all the rest are the oldest. How many oldest children are in the camp?", ans:"40", hint:"First find the number of youngest children, then how many children are left, then the number of middle-group children.", sol:"Youngest: 96 : 8 · 3 = 36. The rest: 96 − 36 = 60. Middle group: 60 : 3 = 20. Oldest: 60 − 20 = 40."},
    {q:"The first worker can do the whole job in 12 hours, and the second in 6 hours. How many hours will it take them to do the job working together?", ans:"4", hint:"Find what fraction of the job each one does in 1 hour, and add these fractions.", sol:"In 1 hour, the first does {1|12} of the job, the second does {1|6}. Together: {1|12} + {2|12} = {3|12} = {1|4} of the job per hour. So the whole job will take 4 hours."},
    {q:"Reduce the fraction {28|63}. The answer should be an irreducible fraction.", ans:"4/9", reduced:true, hint:"Find the largest number that both 28 and 63 are divisible by.", sol:"GCD(28, 63) = 7, so {28|63} = {4|9}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {7|10} − {2|15}.", ans:"17/30", reduced:true, hint:"The common denominator is 30.", sol:"{7|10} = {21|30}, {2|15} = {4|30}. {21|30} − {4|30} = {17|30}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 1{3|5} · 2{1|2}.", ans:"4", hint:"Convert both numbers into improper fractions: {8|5} and {5|2}.", sol:"1{3|5} = {8|5}, 2{1|2} = {5|2}. {8|5} · {5|2} = {40|10} = 4."},
    {q:"A churn holds {5|8} of a bucket of water. {3|4} of the churn was filled. What fraction of a bucket of water was poured in?", ans:"15/32", reduced:true, hint:"Find {3|4} of {5|8}, that is, multiply these fractions.", sol:"{5|8} · {3|4} = {15|32}. This fraction is already irreducible."},
    {q:"For one pie you need {3|4} cup of sugar. How many cups of sugar are needed for 6 such pies? Write the answer as a mixed number.", ans:"4 1/2", hint:"Multiply {3|4} by 6, then pull out the whole-number part.", sol:"{3|4} · 6 = {18|4} = {9|2} = 4{1|2} cups. Answer: 4{1|2}.", reduced:true},
    {q:"A pizza was cut into 8 equal slices. Petya ate {1|4} of the pizza, and Olya ate {3|8} of the pizza. How many slices of pizza are left?", ans:"3", hint:"Express how many slices each one ate: one slice is {1|8} of the pizza.", sol:"{1|4} = {2|8}, so Petya ate 2 slices, Olya ate 3 slices. Together they ate 2 + 3 = 5 slices, leaving 8 − 5 = 3. Answer: 3."},
    {q:"A student added fractions like this: {1|3} + {1|4} = {2|7}. Find the correct value of the sum.", ans:"7/12", hint:"You can't add numerators and denominators separately: first bring the fractions to a common denominator.", sol:"The student added the numerators and denominators separately — that's not allowed. Correct: {1|3} + {1|4} = {4|12} + {3|12} = {7|12}. Answer: {7|12}.", reduced:true},
    {q:"What number must be added to 2{3|8} to get 5? Write the answer as a mixed number.", ans:"2 5/8", hint:"The unknown addend is found by subtraction: 5 − 2{3|8}.", sol:"5 − 2{3|8} = 4{8|8} − 2{3|8} = 2{5|8}. Check: 2{3|8} + 2{5|8} = 4{8|8} = 5. Answer: 2{5|8}.", reduced:true},
    {q:"A lesson lasts {3|4} of an hour, and the break after it lasts {1|6} of an hour. How many minutes do the lesson and the break last together?", ans:"55", hint:"There are 60 minutes in an hour. Find {3|4} of 60 and {1|6} of 60.", sol:"Lesson: 60 : 4 · 3 = 45 min. Break: 60 : 6 = 10 min. Together: 45 + 10 = 55 min. Answer: 55."},
    {q:"How many fractions of the form {n|12}, where n is a natural number, are greater than {1|3} but less than {3|4}? Reducible fractions count too.", ans:"4", hint:"Write {1|3} and {3|4} with denominator 12 and see which numerators lie between them.", sol:"{1|3} = {4|12}, {3|4} = {9|12}. Between them are the fractions {5|12}, {6|12}, {7|12}, {8|12} — that's 4. Answer: 4."},
    {q:"Dima had 600 ₽. He spent {1|3} of the money on a book, and {2|5} of what was left on lunch. How many rubles does he have left?", ans:"240", hint:"Careful: {2|5} is taken not from the 600 ₽, but from what was left after buying the book.", sol:"Book: 600 : 3 = 200 ₽, leaving 600 − 200 = 400 ₽. Lunch: 400 : 5 · 2 = 160 ₽. Left: 400 − 160 = 240 ₽. Answer: 240."},
    {q:"A pedestrian walks at a speed of 4{1|2} km/h. How many kilometers will he cover in 1{1|3} hours?", ans:"6", hint:"Distance = speed · time. Convert the mixed numbers into improper fractions.", sol:"4{1|2} = {9|2}, 1{1|3} = {4|3}. {9|2} · {4|3} = {36|6} = 6 km. Answer: 6."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"What is the sum {1|2} + {1|4} + {1|8} + {1|16}? Notice the pattern.", ans:"15/16", reduced:true, hint:"Each fraction is half of the previous one. Imagine a cake: you eat half, then half of what's left…", sol:"It's short of a whole by {1|16}, so the sum equals 1 − {1|16} = {15|16}."},
  test: [
    {q:"Reduce the fraction {28|42}.", ans:"2/3", reduced:true, sol:"GCD(28, 42) = 14, {28|42} = {2|3}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {7|12} + {1|6}.", ans:"3/4", reduced:true, sol:"{7|12} + {2|12} = {9|12} = {3|4}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {5|9} · {3|10}.", ans:"1/6", reduced:true, sol:"{15|90} = {1|6}."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: {4|5} : {8|15}.", ans:"3/2", reduced:true, sol:"{4|5} · {15|8} = {60|40} = {3|2}."},
    {q:"Which of these fractions is the largest?", opts:["{3|5}","{5|8}","{7|12}","{4|7}"], ans:1, sol:"As decimals: 0.6; 0.625; 0.583…; 0.571… The largest is {5|8}."},
    {q:"Find {3|4} of 36.", ans:"27", sol:"36 : 4 · 3 = 27."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 1{5|8} + 2{1|4}.", ans:"3 7/8", reduced:true, sol:"{5|8} + {1|4} = {5|8} + {2|8} = {7|8}. Whole numbers: 1 + 2 = 3. Total: 3{7|8}."},
    {q:"A pool is filled by two pipes: the first fills {1|5} of the pool per hour, the second fills {3|10} of the pool per hour. What fraction of the pool will both pipes fill in 1 hour working together?", ans:"1/2", reduced:true, sol:"{1|5} + {3|10} = {2|10} + {3|10} = {5|10} = {1|2}."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"An experiment with a sheet of paper", b:"Take a sheet of paper and fold it in half — you get {1|2}. Fold it again — {1|4}. Once more — {1|8}. Unfold the sheet, color in 2 parts out of 8, and compare it with one part out of 4. Make sure that {2|8} = {1|4}. That's how the basic property of a fraction &ldquo;works.&rdquo;"},
    {t:"A kitchen recipe", b:"A pancake recipe for 4 servings needs {3|4} cup of milk. How much milk is needed for 6 servings? How much for 2? Try making real pancakes from the recalculated recipe and check that the batter turns out the same."},
    {t:"Fractions in music", b:"Note durations are fractions: a whole note, {1|2}, {1|4}, {1|8}. In a {4|4} measure, one {1|2} note and two {1|4} notes can fit. Come up with three different {4|4} measures made of notes of different lengths."},
    {t:"Fractions on a kitchen scale", b:"Weigh a cup of grain — say, 200 g. Pour {1|4} of this cup into another container and weigh it separately. Check that you get about 50 g, that is, {1|4} of 200. Repeat for {1|3} and {2|5} and compare the result with the calculation on paper."}
  ]
};

