CONTENT_EN.frac.steps = [
  {title:"What a fraction is", body:
    "<p>A fraction {a|b} shows this: the whole was divided into <b>b</b> equal parts, and <b>a</b> of them were taken. The number below the line is the <b>denominator</b>, the number above the line is the <b>numerator</b>.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_PIE__</div>",
    exIdx:[8]},
  {title:"The basic property of a fraction", body:
    "<p>If the numerator and denominator are multiplied (or divided) by the same number that isn't zero, the fraction doesn't change: {1|2} = {2|4} = {5|10}. Dividing by a common divisor is called <b>reducing</b>: {18|24} = {3|4} (divided by 6).</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_STRIPS__</div><p>Look at the &ldquo;wall of fractions&rdquo;: all the strips are the same length. The dashed line in the middle shows that {1|2}, {2|4}, {3|6}, and {6|12} cover the same part of the strip, that is, {1|2} = {2|4} = {3|6} = {6|12}. So a fraction can be written in different ways while its value stays the same.</p>",
    exIdx:[0,5]},
  {title:"Adding and subtracting fractions", body:
    "<p><b>Addition and subtraction.</b> First find a common denominator, then do the operation with the numerators: {1|2} + {1|3} = {3|6} + {2|6} = {5|6}.</p><p>Watch how it works:</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_ADD_ANIM__</div>",
    exIdx:[1,6]},
  {title:"Multiplying fractions", body:
    "<p><b>Multiplication.</b> Numerator times numerator, denominator times denominator: {2|3} · {3|4} = {6|12} = {1|2}.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC5_MULT__</div>",
    exIdx:[7]},
  {title:"Dividing fractions", body:
    "<p><b>Division.</b> We multiply by the &ldquo;flipped&rdquo; fraction: {2|3} : {4|5} = {2|3} · {5|4} = {10|12} = {5|6}.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC5_DIV__</div>",
    exIdx:[2]},
  {title:"Mixed numbers", body:
    "<p>1{3|4} is 1 + {3|4}. To convert to an improper fraction: (1 · 4 + 3) / 4 = {7|4}. Before multiplying and dividing, we always convert mixed numbers into improper fractions.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_FRAC_NUMLINE__</div><p>Fractions have their place on the number line too: {1|2} sits exactly halfway between 0 and 1, while 1{3|4} sits between 1 and 2, closer to 2. In the diagram for 1{3|4}, you can see two parts: the whole-number part is 1, the fractional part is {3|4}, that is, 1{3|4} = 1 + {3|4} = {7|4}. The point 1{1|4} is one tick mark to the right of 1.</p>",
    exIdx:[3]},
  {title:"Common mistakes when adding fractions", body:
    "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>You can only add and subtract fractions that have the same denominator.</div><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>{1|2} + {1|3} = {2|5} — you can't do it this way. A common-sense check: {2|5} is less than {1|2}, but a sum can't be smaller than one of its addends.</div>",
    exIdx:[4]}
];

/* ---------- dec ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.dec = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to add, subtract, multiply, and divide decimal fractions just as confidently as whole numbers, and easily convert them to common fractions and back.",
  explain: "<p>A decimal fraction is a common fraction with a denominator of 10, 100, 1000, and so on, written with a decimal point. For example, {7|10} = 0.7; {35|100} = 0.35; {125|1000} = 0.125. The digits after the decimal point are places: the first digit is tenths, the second is hundredths, the third is thousandths.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_DEC_PLACE__</div>"
  + "<p>Let's break down the number 3.405 by its place-value table: 3 — ones, 4 — tenths, 0 — hundredths, 5 — thousandths. So 3.405 = 3 + {4|10} + {0|100} + {5|1000}. Notice that the zero in the hundredths place also holds its spot — without it you'd get a completely different number (3.45).</p>"
  + "<p>Here's an even clearer way to see it: take a square made of 100 little cells.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_DEC_GRID__</div>"
  + "<p>3 full columns are shaded (that's 30 cells) plus 5 more cells — 35 out of 100 in total. That's {35|100} = 0.35 (and 35%: more on that in the topic about percentages).</p>"
  + "<h3>Addition and subtraction</h3>"
  + "<p>Decimal fractions are added and subtracted in <b>column form</b>: decimal point under decimal point, filling empty spots after the point with zeros. Watch:</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_DEC_COLUMN_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<p>3.40 + 2.15 = 5.55; 7.80 − 4.35 = 3.45.</p>"
  + "<h3>Multiplication</h3>"
  + "<p>Decimal fractions are multiplied like whole numbers, ignoring the decimal point, and then in the answer, counting from the right, you mark off as many digits as there were after the decimal point in both factors combined. 2.4 · 3 = 24 · 3 = 72, there was one digit after the point — so the answer is 7.2.</p>"
  + "<h3>Division</h3>"
  + "<p>To divide by a decimal fraction, move the decimal point in both the dividend and the divisor the same number of places to the right, so the divisor becomes a whole number. 4.8 : 1.2 — move the decimal point 1 place in both numbers: 48 : 12 = 4.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_NUMLINE__</div>"
  + "<p>On the number line, decimal fractions sit between whole numbers: 0.7 lies between 0 and 1, closer to 1, and 2.25 lies between 2 and 3, closer to 2.</p>"
  + "<h3>Converting to a common fraction and back</h3>"
  + "<p>To write a decimal fraction as a common one, read it aloud: &ldquo;zero point thirty-five hundredths&rdquo; = {35|100} = {7|20} (reduced by 5). To turn a common fraction into a decimal, divide the numerator by the denominator in column form: {3|4} = 3 : 4 = 0.75.</p>"
  + "<h3>Rounding</h3>"
  + "<p>To round a number to a given place, look at the next digit after it: if it's 5 or more, increase the place's digit by 1; if it's less than 5, leave it unchanged; and drop all the digits after it. Let's round 4.673 to the tenths: the next digit is 7, so we increase 6 to 7: we get 4.7.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_DEC_ROUND__</div>"
  + "<p>The diagram shows a zoomed-in piece of the number line between 4.6 and 4.8. The number 4.673 lies to the right of the boundary 4.65 (the midpoint between 4.6 and 4.7), so it's closer to 4.7. The &ldquo;5 or more&rdquo; rule is exactly this boundary: to the left of 4.65 we round down to 4.6, to the right we round up to 4.7.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Zeros at the end of a decimal fraction, after the decimal point, can be added or removed — this doesn't change the number's value: 3.4 = 3.40 = 3.400.</div>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>When adding 3.4 + 2.15 in column form, some people add them &ldquo;as whole numbers,&rdquo; without aligning the decimal point, and get a wrong result like 3.55. Always fill in zeros so there's the same number of digits after the decimal point: 3.40 + 2.15 = 5.55.</div>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 4.35 + 2.7.", s:"Equalize the number of digits after the decimal point: 2.7 = 2.70. Add in column form: 4.35 + 2.70 = 7.05."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 8.4 − 3.65.", s:"Equalize: 8.4 = 8.40. Subtract in column form: 8.40 − 3.65 = 4.75."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 2.5 · 1.2.", s:"Multiply as whole numbers: 25 · 12 = 300. After the decimal point in both factors there are 1 + 1 = 2 digits in total, so in the answer we mark off 2 digits from the right: 3.00 = 3."},
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 7.2 : 0.6.", s:"Move the decimal point in the dividend and divisor 1 place to the right: 72 : 6 = 12."},
    {t:"Write 0.35 as a common fraction and reduce it.", s:"0.35 = {35|100}. GCD(35, 100) = 5. Reduce: {35|100} = {7|20}."},
    {t:"Find the mistake. A student wrote: 0.3 + 0.25 = 0.28. Find the correct value of the expression.", s:"The student added 3 and 25 as whole numbers, but 3 tenths and 25 hundredths are different places. Write 0.3 = 0.30 and add with the decimal points aligned: 0.30 + 0.25 = 0.55. The mistake could have been spotted right away: a sum can't be smaller than an addend, and 0.28 < 0.3."},
    {t:"Arrange the numbers in increasing order: 0.7; 0.68; 0.705; 0.6.", s:"Equalize the number of digits after the decimal point: 0.700; 0.680; 0.705; 0.600. Now compare them as whole numbers: 600 < 680 < 700 < 705. Answer: 0.6 < 0.68 < 0.7 < 0.705. Trap: the number 0.68 isn't bigger than 0.7 just because &ldquo;68 is bigger than 7.&rdquo;"},
    {t:"Restore the digit: 3.□5 + 1.4 = 4.95.", s:"Let's find the first addend: 4.95 − 1.4 = 4.95 − 1.40 = 3.55. So the digit 5 needs to go in the box. Check: 3.55 + 1.4 = 4.95."},
    {t:"A kilogram of apples costs 68.5 ₽. Mom bought 1.2 kg and paid 100 ₽. How many rubles of change did she get?", s:"Cost of the purchase: 68.5 · 1.2 = 82.2 ₽ (685 · 12 = 8220, mark off 2 digits). Change: 100 − 82.2 = 17.8 ₽. As a single expression: 100 − 68.5 · 1.2."},
    {t:"Express in kilograms and add: 4 kg 60 g and 2 kg 5 g.", s:"1 g = 0.001 kg. So 60 g = 0.06 kg and 4 kg 60 g = 4.06 kg; 5 g = 0.005 kg and 2 kg 5 g = 2.005 kg. Sum: 4.060 + 2.005 = 6.065 kg."}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 3.4 + 2.15.", ans:"5.55", hint:"Equalize the number of digits after the decimal point: 3.4 = 3.40.", sol:"3.40 + 2.15 = 5.55."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 7.8 − 4.35.", ans:"3.45", hint:"Write 7.8 as 7.80.", sol:"7.80 − 4.35 = 3.45."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 12.6 + 3.47.", ans:"16.07", hint:"12.6 = 12.60, add in column form.", sol:"12.60 + 3.47 = 16.07."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 9 − 4.25.", ans:"4.75", hint:"Think of 9 as 9.00.", sol:"9.00 − 4.25 = 4.75."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 0.6 · 0.5.", ans:"0.3", hint:"Multiply 6 by 5, then mark off 2 digits from the right (one from each factor).", sol:"6 · 5 = 30. Mark off 2 digits: 0.30 = 0.3."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 4.8 : 1.2.", ans:"4", hint:"Move the decimal point in both numbers 1 place to the right.", sol:"48 : 12 = 4."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 15 : 0.5.", ans:"30", hint:"Move the decimal point in both numbers 1 place: 150 : 5.", sol:"150 : 5 = 30."},
    {q:"Write the fraction {3|4} as a decimal fraction.", ans:"0.75", hint:"Divide the numerator by the denominator in column form: 3 : 4.", sol:"3 : 4 = 0.75."},
    {q:"Write the decimal fraction 0.6 as a common fraction (in lowest terms).", ans:"3/5", reduced:true, hint:"0.6 = {6|10}, reduce by a common factor.", sol:"0.6 = {6|10} = {3|5} (reduced by 2)."},
    {q:"Round the number 4.673 to the tenths.", ans:"4.7", hint:"Look at the hundredths digit — it decides whether to increase the tenths digit.", sol:"After the tenths comes the digit 7 (≥5), so we increase the tenths: 4.673 ≈ 4.7."},
    {q:"Round the number 0.847 to the hundredths.", ans:"0.85", hint:"Look at the thousandths digit.", sol:"After the hundredths comes 7 (≥5), so we increase the hundredths: 0.847 ≈ 0.85."},
    {q:"At a store, 3 pens at 12.5 ₽ each and a notebook for 34.9 ₽ were bought. How many rubles were paid in total?", ans:"72.4", hint:"First find the cost of three pens, then add the notebook.", sol:"3 · 12.5 = 37.5. Total: 37.5 + 34.9 = 72.4 ₽."},
    {q:"A ribbon 2.4 m long was cut into pieces of 0.3 m each. How many pieces were there?", ans:"8", hint:"Divide the total length by the length of one piece.", sol:"2.4 : 0.3 = 24 : 3 = 8 pieces."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 3.75 + 1.25 · 2.", ans:"6.25", hint:"Multiplication first, then addition — order of operations works with decimal fractions too.", sol:"1.25 · 2 = 2.5. Then 3.75 + 2.5 = 6.25."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 8.4 − 2.5 · 1.6.", ans:"4.4", hint:"Multiplication first: 2.5 · 1.6.", sol:"2.5 · 1.6 = 4.00 = 4. Then 8.4 − 4 = 4.4."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: (4.5 + 3.5) : 0.4.", ans:"20", hint:"First add the numbers in the parentheses.", sol:"4.5 + 3.5 = 8. Then 8 : 0.4 = 80 : 4 = 20."},
    {q:"A car drove for 3.5 hours at a speed of 64.4 km/h. What distance did it cover?", ans:"225.4", hint:"Distance equals speed times time.", sol:"64.4 · 3.5 = 225.4 km."},
    {q:"One cake needs 0.75 kg of flour. How many kilograms of flour are needed for 2.5 such cakes (a recipe scaled up 2.5 times)?", ans:"1.875", hint:"Multiply the amount of flour for 1 cake by 2.5.", sol:"0.75 · 2.5 = 1.875 kg."},
    {q:"Katya had 45.6 ₽. She bought 3 identical pins at 8.75 ₽ each. How many rubles does she have left?", ans:"19.35", hint:"First find how much all the pins cost, then subtract from the total amount.", sol:"3 · 8.75 = 26.25. Left: 45.6 − 26.25 = 19.35 ₽."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: (12.5 + 7.5) · 0.6 − 4.8, then round the result to the nearest whole number.", ans:"7", hint:"First carry out the whole expression step by step, and do the rounding as the last step.", sol:"12.5 + 7.5 = 20. 20 · 0.6 = 12. 12 − 4.8 = 7.2. Round 7.2 to the nearest whole number: after the decimal point there's a 2 (<5), so we round down: 7.2 ≈ 7."},
    {q:"2 kg 300 g of apples, 1.85 kg of pears, and 900 g of plums were picked. How many kilograms of fruit were picked in total?", ans:"5.05", hint:"First convert everything to kilograms: 300 g = 0.3 kg, 900 g = 0.9 kg.", sol:"2 kg 300 g = 2.3 kg; 900 g = 0.9 kg. Sum: 2.3 + 1.85 + 0.9 = 5.05 kg."},
    {q:"Find the number x, if (x − 1.2) · 0.5 = 3.4.", ans:"8", hint:"First find what the difference x − 1.2 equals: it's multiplied by 0.5.", sol:"x − 1.2 = 3.4 : 0.5 = 6.8. Then x = 6.8 + 1.2 = 8. Check: (8 − 1.2) · 0.5 = 6.8 · 0.5 = 3.4."},
    {q:"A student calculated: 0.6 · 0.7 = 4.2. Find the correct value of the product.", ans:"0.42", hint:"First multiply 6 by 7, then count how many digits after the decimal point there are in the two factors combined.", sol:"6 · 7 = 42. Each factor has one digit after the decimal point, 2 in total, so the answer also has 2 digits: 0.42. The answer 4.2 can't be correct: two numbers less than 1 give a product smaller than either of them."},
    {q:"How many numbers of the form a.b exist (a is the whole part, b is one digit after the decimal point; a form like 3.0 is allowed) that are greater than 2.35 but less than 3.08?", ans:"7", hint:"List such numbers in order, starting from the smallest one greater than 2.35.", sol:"These numbers work: 2.4; 2.5; 2.6; 2.7; 2.8; 2.9; 3.0. The number 2.3 is less than 2.35, and 3.1 is greater than 3.08. 7 numbers in total."},
    {q:"A car uses 7.5 L of gasoline per 100 km. Gasoline costs 52.4 ₽ per liter. How many rubles does the gasoline cost for a 240 km trip?", ans:"943.2", hint:"First find how many liters are needed for 240 km, that is, how many times 100 km fits into 240 km.", sol:"240 km is 2.4 times 100 km. Consumption: 7.5 · 2.4 = 18 L. Cost: 18 · 52.4 = 943.2 ₽."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 6.08 + 3.7.", ans:"9.78", hint:"Equalize the number of digits after the decimal point: 3.7 = 3.70.", sol:"6.08 + 3.70 = 9.78."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 12 − 5.45.", ans:"6.55", hint:"Think of 12 as 12.00.", sol:"12.00 − 5.45 = 6.55."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 3.6 · 0.25.", ans:"0.9", hint:"Multiply 36 by 25, then count how many digits after the decimal point there are in both factors.", sol:"36 · 25 = 900. The factors have 1 + 2 = 3 digits after the decimal point in total, so the answer also has 3: 0.900 = 0.9."},
    {q:"A jar of honey weighs 0.65 kg. How many kilograms do 8 such jars weigh?", ans:"5.2", hint:"Multiply the mass of one jar by the number of jars.", sol:"0.65 · 8 = 5.2 kg."},
    {q:"Mom bought 2.5 kg of apples at 84 ₽ per kilogram and gave the seller 500 ₽. How many rubles of change did she receive?", ans:"290", hint:"First find the cost of the purchase: the price per 1 kg times the number of kilograms.", sol:"84 · 2.5 = 210 ₽. Change: 500 − 210 = 290 ₽. Answer: 290."},
    {q:"A student calculated: 5.2 − 1.35 = 4.15. Find the correct value of the difference.", ans:"3.85", hint:"Write in the zero: 5.2 = 5.20 — and subtract in column form, borrowing from the neighboring place.", sol:"5.20 − 1.35: hundredths 0 − 5 — borrow, 10 − 5 = 5; tenths 1 − 3 — borrow, 11 − 3 = 8; ones 4 − 1 = 3. We get 3.85. The student subtracted the smaller digit from the larger one without borrowing. Answer: 3.85."},
    {q:"A bottle holds 1.5 L of juice. How many such bottles are needed to pour out 21 L of juice?", ans:"14", hint:"Divide 21 by 1.5, first moving the decimal point in both numbers one place to the right.", sol:"21 : 1.5 = 210 : 15 = 14 bottles. Answer: 14."},
    {q:"A runner ran 100 m in 12.46 seconds. Round his result to the nearest tenth of a second.", ans:"12.5", hint:"Look at the hundredths digit: it decides whether to change the tenths digit.", sol:"The hundredths digit is 6 (≥ 5), so we increase the tenths digit by 1: 12.46 ≈ 12.5. Answer: 12.5."},
    {q:"Write the mixed number 2{3|8} as a decimal fraction.", ans:"2.375", hint:"The whole part stays whole, and get {3|8} by dividing 3 : 8 in column form.", sol:"3 : 8 = 0.375. So 2{3|8} = 2 + 0.375 = 2.375. Answer: 2.375."},
    {q:"What number must 0.4 be multiplied by to get 3.2?", ans:"8", hint:"An unknown factor is found by division: 3.2 : 0.4.", sol:"3.2 : 0.4 = 32 : 4 = 8. Check: 0.4 · 8 = 3.2. Answer: 8."},
    {q:"A garden bed has the shape of a rectangle with a length of 3.5 m and a width of 1.2 m. Find its area (in m^2).", ans:"4.2", hint:"The area of a rectangle is the length multiplied by the width. Don't forget to count the digits after the decimal point.", sol:"35 · 12 = 420. After the decimal point in the factors there are 1 + 1 = 2 digits: 4.20 = 4.2 m^2. Answer: 4.2."},
    {q:"A taxi ride costs 150 ₽ for pickup plus 24.5 ₽ for each kilometer. How many rubles does a 12 km ride cost?", ans:"444", hint:"First find the charge for 12 kilometers, then add the pickup fee.", sol:"24.5 · 12 = 294 ₽. Total: 150 + 294 = 444 ₽. Answer: 444."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"Replace the product 0.25 · 0.25 · 16 with the shortest mental-math approach and find its value.", ans:"1", hint:"Remember that 0.25 = {1|4}. Multiplying by {1|4} twice is the same as dividing by 16.", sol:"0.25 · 0.25 = {1|4} · {1|4} = {1|16}. Then {1|16} · 16 = 1."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 5.6 + 3.45.", ans:"9.05", sol:"5.60 + 3.45 = 9.05."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 10 − 6.35.", ans:"3.65", sol:"10.00 − 6.35 = 3.65."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 1.5 · 0.4.", ans:"0.6", sol:"15 · 4 = 60, mark off 2 digits: 0.60 = 0.6."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression: 9.6 : 1.2.", ans:"8", sol:"96 : 12 = 8."},
    {q:"Write {1|8} as a decimal fraction.", ans:"0.125", sol:"1 : 8 = 0.125."},
    {q:"What number do you get if 0.45 is rounded to the tenths?", opts:["0.4","0.5","0.45","0.40"], ans:1, sol:"After the tenths comes the digit 5, so we increase the tenths: 0.45 ≈ 0.5."},
    {q:"Write the decimal fraction 1.25 as a common fraction in lowest terms.", ans:"5/4", reduced:true, sol:"1.25 = {125|100} = {5|4} (reduced by 25)."},
    {q:"A carton of milk costs 74.5 ₽. How much will 4 such cartons cost?", ans:"298", sol:"74.5 · 4 = 298 ₽."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"A supermarket receipt", b:"Take a real receipt or look up prices in a store app. Add the prices of five items in column form on paper, aligning the decimal point, and compare it with the total on the receipt — they should match."},
    {t:"Weighing on kitchen scales", b:"Weigh a few objects (an apple, a spoon, a notebook) on kitchen scales — they usually show the weight in kilograms with decimal digits, for example 0.15 kg. Add the weights of three objects in column form and check the result by putting them all together on the scale."},
    {t:"Stopwatch and rounding", b:"Time on your phone how long it takes you to tie your shoelaces — the stopwatch will show thousandths of a second, for example 14.873 s. Round the result first to the tenths, then to the nearest whole second, and compare how much more precise rounding to the tenths is."},
    {t:"A ruler and common fractions", b:"Measure the length of a pencil with a ruler to the nearest millimeter, and write the result in centimeters as a decimal fraction (for example, 17.6 cm). Convert this decimal fraction into a common fraction of a centimeter and reduce it."}
  ]
};

